        <p>1 '</p>
        <p>i :</p>
        <p>consult with them; but he could assure the meeting that Widnes would be still in this busindss along with Warrington and St. Helens as much as they could. He agreed that War- rington should be made the centre, and it gave him great pleasure to be with them that day. (Applause.) Rev. Canon Stevenson asked if an adopted prisoner would mean, a cost of £2 2s. per month in future, as against £1 at present,. The Mayor said that was so. Alderman Bennett agreed with the previous speakers as to forming a Regimental Care Com- mittee with Warrington as the centre. If more help were needed he was sure it was only a matter of appealing to the women of Warring- ton .Ard the help would be given. He moved that the. scheme he adopted. Rev. Canon Stevenson seconded. If their' present privilege were taken away it would rob them of a great deal of interest and pride in the men who had suffered SO much for their I sake. (Applause.) The proposal was accepted by all present. His Worship then referred to the fact that the work of organisation up to the present had been always done by Mr. T. H. Spinks. (Ap- plause.) Mr. Spinks had now taken on additional work in connection with the war pensions, and his hands were full and he worked very late. Under this new scheme there would be a lot more clerical work. They would re- quire either voluntary assistance or make a paid appointment. This matter was held over for consideration by the Care Committee. . The Mayor further mentioned that owing to the increased number of parcels which would have to be made up the Parr Hall could not be used half as much as it is for other pur- poses. They could not hitve a concert at night and then commence making up parcels next morning. This concluded The 'meeting.</p>
