      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WORKMEN'S CARDS.</head>
        <p>WORKMEN'S CARDS.</p>
        <p>Chairman Of Munitions' Cour</p>
        <p>Gives A Lead.</p>
        <p>A very interesting case came before the Munitions Court on Monday, when a com- plaint; formulated against four of their employes by a prominent local firm of wire manufacturers, came on for hearing. Joseph Gallop, Samuel Harrison, John Hilton and Joseph Hinchliffe, employed in. the anneaLing department, were charged with having on the 29th October neglected their work by playing nards during working hours. The men admitted playing a game of cards, but denied that they had neglected their work.—Mr. George Seabury, of the National Union of General Workers, appeared for the men., The firm's works ziaanager gave evidence to the effect that on Sunday, 29th October, at twenty minutes past eleven, a.m., he passed through the annealing department where de- fendants should have been at work. , He looked around and subsequently foand them in a place where wire is stored, playing cards. He said. "This sort of thing is not right, especially at the present time," and each replied that he was sorry.</p>
        <p>Mi. Seabury cross-examined witness with the object of showing that the work was not delayed in any way, as the process through which the material was passing occupied three hours, and their_ portion of the task had been completed. He also asked if that week did not show the greatest production from the "pots" during October, and witness replied that lie was not aware.</p>
        <p>Evidence for the defence was to the effect that at the time in question the tasks necessary on that Sunday morning had been completed, and that there was no other work that the men could have done. An interesting fact trans- pired that the shift on which the men were employed lasted from 5.30 p.m. on the Saturday night until one p.m. on Sunday. The firm's representative said the men could lave the shift altered if Hwy wished. Mr. Ridgway to defendants (after consulting with the assessors): This is the first case of the kind brought before us here, and what I wish to emphasise most strongly is this—that you had no business whatever to play :cards in the works. If any other cases of playing cards in works come before me thero will be g heavy fine imposed. We are disposed fo -think that practically you did Jour work, ap4 tag (taus will be amassed with a -caution. Chorus of defendants: Thank you, sir,</p>
        <p>Journalistic Tact. "We much regret that the report given last week as to Private -----, having died of wounds, was incorrect."—" Alloa Advertiser."</p>
        <p>Forme MEALS Thirty drops of Mother Seigel's Syrup, taken after meals, will prevent of remove pains after eating. Try it also for Indigestion, biliousness, headaches, and constipation. It is the world's r emedy for stomach and liver troubles. IT).‘KE MOTHER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SEIGEL'S SYRUP mtminim</head>
        <p>SEIGEL'S SYRUP mtminim</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Delusions.</p>
        <p>At a local inquest last week it was stated that the deceased was sometimes under the de- lusion that a man and a boy followed him to the door and shouted after him. It brought to my mind an incident of more than 30 years ago. In the town in which I then lived, a blind man regularly stood in the street on market days, selling braces, garters and laces, the stock being hung about his person. He lived miles out in the country and had a lonely as well as late walk home; and eventually the trying ex- perience got on the poor fellow's nerves. One Monday morning, at the County Police Court, he complained to the magistrates of having on the previous Saturday night been followed along the road by mysterious persons who poured into his ear terrifying threats. He admitted he was not robbed or actually assaulted, and it was evident that he suffered from delusions. Shorthand Notes.</p>
        <p>The Borough Court case in which a young lady produced what purported to be a shorthand note of certain proceedings reminded me of ex- periences of my own at a County Court which I attended years ago. The then Judge, since deceased, one day made a perfectly gratuitous attack on the fraternity. Counsel in what seemed likely to be a lengthy case suggested that his Hbnour should order a special short- hand , note, and the Judge, in declining, said his experience was that men engaged on. such work were generally, unable to transcribe their notes. On a subsequent occasion of the kind; the same Judge ' asked counsel to "see if arrangements could not be made with one of these gentlemen (beaming genially on the representatives of the local Press) with a view Ito a shorthand note." Counsel turned to me with a question as to the fee, and, his Honour's public insult still rankling, I took care to quote high, in the hope that tliv Judge wouli not _ . . obtain the, relief he sought Counsel declared it to be quite beyond his client's means, and when, in answer to a question fiom the Bench, he repeated the sum mentioned, the Judge ex- claimed "I eould get a man from London to do it for less than that." And one reporter murmured "As your Honour pleases." •</p>
        <p>Something Like Reason. The parrot story on page two of the last issue recalled an adventure which I may possibly have mentioned before—l don't remember. Visiting the Zoo well into the second generation ago, I stopped to look at a fine parrot. A stranger came along at the moment and said "Morning, Polly," and Polly returned the salutation quite genially. "Morning, Polly," the man repeated, and Polly, having apparently sized him up and yet wishing to be polite, re- plied in a distinctly bored tone, "Morning." " Morning, Polly," persisted her persecutor, whereupon the bird screamed resentfully. "Oh, go 'way, go 'way." Apparently dumb with astonishment, the fellow went 'way, went 'way. In Court Dress.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather's plea that he would be unable to accompany the Mayor to church as he had not got any long trousers met with the journalistic assurance that his Worship would doubtless be pleased to see him in knee breeches. I was carried back in memory to a period when the then Mayor of a borough in which I sojourned had the honour of presentation at Court. At a banquet following his Worship's -re-election to the mayoralty, he stood at the head of the wide staircase in full Court regalia, sword of course included, to welcome and dazzle the eyes of his ,guests—a sight for the gods. Since I be- gan. to write this weekly, column, the good man, whose vanity amused many and hurt none, has passed away.</p>
        <p>"After Only Three Months." , The account of Private F. Longshaw's unfor- tunate end "after spending only three months in France" set me. thinking of many things. .1</p>
        <p>was personally acquainted with two boys who were killed immediately, on entering into their first fight; but the phrase quoted in my headline reminds me particularly of a lad who was sent out after only three months' training, and was -killed in his first engagement. This appears to have been a not uncommon fate, and one cannot help contrasting it with some others: A young fellow who was on the staff of the last paper on which I worked, and who has completed his training, writes me from a camp in the South- East of England that some of his comrades have been in training a couple of years and that they are all "fed up." Then there—was the case of the Manchester Pals on the East Coast, It is all very puzzling to the lay mind, but no doubt, as a certain advertisement bath it, "There's a reason." "A Dismal Find."</p>
        <p>At a Walton inquest last week, a witness said: "I went up and spoke •to it,' but it did not answer. It was a dead body." My youngest brother, had the like startling adventure in Lon- don when we were children. We lived in a Court off the Strand, and he was given to ex- ploring the Adelphi Dark Arches. On one of his visits, when he had advanced but a few yards from the entrance, he saw a fine figure of a man standing bolt upright against the wall. Struck by his singular appearance, my brother shouted to the motionless one, and. getting no answer, ventured near and touched 'him, upon which he fell forward and Nyas found to be lifeless- He had died in the position in which he was first notice&amp;</p>
        <p>Domestic Virtues (?) The amusing story sent by a Lymm soldier and reproduced in last week's distrkt notes re- minded me of that of the Hindoo who, dying, presented himself at the gates of Paradise, and, challenged by Brahma with "Hest been to Purgatory?" admitted that he had not, but successfully urged that he had been married many years, being thereupon bidden to enter, as having suffered purgatory enough.- Another claimant who had observed this stepped confi- dently forward. " Hast been to Purgatory?" was likewise demanded of him, and, while answering in the negative, he triumphantly added "I have been married twice," where- upon Brahma thundered "Begone! We have no room for fools in Paradise."</p>
        <p>The Angels At Mons. The Rev-. T. T. James' sermon at Wycliffe Congregational Church discloses a lingering wistfulness in relation to the alleged super- natural interposition in behalf of our troops at Mons. How we should like to believe that "superhuman powers " were indeed "exerted for the Allies in retreat "—that, in Shakes- pearean phrase, angels and ministers of grace defended them. Happy thought! Let one of the poets have his say. Hear, for instance, Broome—</p>
        <p>"Thus when angel views (T. Atkins, say) dis- tress'd lie feels compassion pleading in his breast; Instant the heavenly guardian cleaves the skies, And, pleased to save, on wings of lightning • flies." I hfive titlcep slight liberty with it, but it may pp.tss,</p>
        <p>diwistrnas Pudding. Does any wonder at the importance attached to this big .business, and at the .assumption that our gallant fighting men abroad as well as the stay-at-homes must have plum pudding at Christmas? It is a matter of heredity. The custom is of immemorial antiqUity;. it is in our very blood. It marks our way of keeping Christmas. And we mustkeep Christmas, if for no other or better reason than that we have always done .so—except during the short period when the austere and mistaken Puritans vainly tried to abolish it. In 1085 William kept Christmas in great State, as did his successors—Rufus, the Henrys, the Edwards, and the Richards. They did not fail to remember the poor. There was no stint on the part of these right royal providers of feasts. I have been dipping into history, with this point specially in view, and I find that our jolly ancestors had a stomach, not only for fighting—and they did plenty of it—but for 'Christmas fare; and they took care that their dependents enjoyed it, too. Yee, Mr. T.A. must have his Christmas pudding as usual—and readers of "The Examiner" among multitudes of others are going to see that he gets it. -</p>
        <p>The Bishop of London's statement that he could make a speech on behalf of hospitals in his sleep has greatly interested a certain miser of our acquaintance, who says that under simi- lar conditions he is sure he could make a liberal contribution to the Bishop's riuid.—" Punch!!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1916.</p>
        <p>PRISONERS OF WAR.</p>
        <p>Special Care Of _South Lancs.</p>
        <p>Men To Be Maintained.</p>
        <p>ACTIVITIES ENLARGED.</p>
        <p>A specially convened meeting of subscribers, helpers and others interested in the welfare of the above was held in the Town Hall on Wed- nesday afternoon to decide on future action under the new scheme for improving the con- dition of men in captivity.</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) presided, and he was stipported by the Mayor of Widnes (Mr. Sohn Hedley), Colonel Ireland- Blackburne, Sir David Gamble, the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson, Alderman Bennett and others, and ,there was a large number of ladies present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>WHAT THE PAST HAS BEEN.</head>
        <p>WHAT THE PAST HAS BEEN.</p>
        <p>His Worship having stated that Colonel Mog- gridge could not be present, having to attend a Court of Inquiry, explained that the meeting followed on the proposal to co-ordinate the relief of prisoners of war. The South Lancashire Regiment, he ex- plained, is recruited mainly from St. Helens, Warrington and Widnes, Warrington being the depot town. When the needs of the prisoners became known the present organisation was commenced, and it had been carried on very successfully. The number of parcels of food dispatched to 'the men of the regiment was 9,024, the weight of the materials being ,35 tons. In addition over 10 tons of clothing and boots were supplied during the past winter, and a similar quantity was now in course of dis- patch. To enable the local committee to carry on it was found necessary to have a system of "adoption," which consisted in obtaining ." Godparents " for the soldiers. This had re- lieved the funds to a considerable extent of the, total co,.t involved, as all the_versons with whom Men litad been placed guaranteed to send .a certain proportion of parcels per month per Irian. The figures given above did not 'include these. Many, also, of the large organisations' in the country had assisted, as the South Lancashire Regiment was amongst the original Expeditionary Force in the memorable retreat ' from Mons, and the casualties 'in prisoners were necessarily very heavy. The call on their ' resources had been -a longer and heavier one than that of many regiments which had since been raised for the British Army. The weekly cost is now about £4O to- Warrington. In addition to this St. Helens had taken on all its own residents, whether belonging to this or other regiments; and Liverpool, London, Birmingham, Wolverhampton and other towns had been furnished with and had taken care of men belonging to their own districts., They had even got so far afield as to obtain regular payments from places so widely removed from Warrington as Fort Saskatchewan (Alberta), Vancouver, France, Switzerland and Ireland. The object originally in view was to provide every man with a weekly parcel of food. Later it was found desirable to increase this, and as occasion had presented itself endeavours had been made to obtain for each man additional parcels to the total extent of six to eight per month, and arrangements were just on foot for obtaining two parcels per week from "adopters" and friends for every man.</p>
        <p>The New Co-ordination Scheme.</p>
        <p>This now all goes by the board, and the scheme which originated with the new Central Prisoners of War Committee of the British Red Cross Society and Order of St. John of Jerusalem will take its place on the Ist of December.</p>
        <p>His Worship admitted that there was need for some change_ It had been proved to the satisfaction of headquarters that those men who are adepts in the art of cadging succeed at present in obtaining more food than they need, and whilst this would not have been a crime had the extra gone to their less fortu- nate companions, they were informed that in some cases it had been sold to the German guards. Proceeding to slightly outline the new scheme, His Worship said that every regiment would have a Care Committee—the Warrington Committee is already in existence. If authorised by the Central Committee, it could also continue to• pack and dispatch parcels to prisoners. If it did not seek to carry on the work itself it must allot its prisoners by battalions to authorised organisations, and it must be responsible for the roflefetbn of funds for the prisoners in its care. No bread would be allowed to be sent except through Switzer- land or Copenhagen, orders being given by the Respmental Supply Committee at their expense. The cost of this alone would be over £2O a week. Organisations might also be authorised to pack parcels' for prisoners of a regiment either as a whole regiment or in battalions, and if they did so , they must send at least three parcels of food of 101 b, each gross weight to' every 'prisoner in their charge every fort- night. Special labels will be used for the purpose, and this will, to a great extent, do away with the censorship Nthich at present is necessary to avoid prohibited articles being • sent out of the country. Relatives and adopters who had been sending food would be allowed to continue to send certain articles only, but not food or articles set out in a to be pub- lished in the various Post Offices. They are advised to send money through the Care Com- mittees, and the parcels would then be sent out in their names, providing they "adopt" a particular prisoner, which means the expensw of six parcels per month—each parcel costing about 6s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>What The Scheme Will Cost.</head>
        <p>What The Scheme Will Cost.</p>
        <p>To carry out the above scheme so far as Warrington was concerned would, said His Worship, necessitate an expenditure for the men at present on their list of about 11200 a week, this being exclusive of the cost of any staffs which it might be necessary to get if they found there .was not enough help volun- tarily. He thought they would be empowered to establish branches in their own district, and Lady Gamble and Mrs. Hammill, who were in charge of the association in St. Helens and had taken on some of their men, had had a meeting of their subscribers and had decided to offer to undertake the care of 200 South Lancashire men, should it he decided to go on. (Applause.)' Whatever the decisign of the meeting was, he would suggest that there was no difficulty in St. Helena carrying on under the Central Committee should it be decided to hand over their men and continue to collect funds only. He now asked the meeting to say whether Warrington would continue to act as the Regimental Care Committee and also as an authorised Packing Association, and, if so, what number of branch organisations it would be necessary to establish in the outlying districts to help in the work? Should it be decided to carry on, he pointed out that if they got into financial difficulties they could obtain some help from headquarters. They were all, he was sure, agreed on one point, and that was to take such action as would be for the whole and sole benefit of the men, in enemy countries. (Applause.) kHe was not at liberty to say how many men they had to provide for; but they .could draw their , own conclusions when St. Helens offered to take 200. They all supported the idea of a Regimental \Care Com- mittee from a 'sentimental point of view, but it would necessitate about 20 or 30 more ladies to aastt 4very week, and they would also have to consider the question of having a clerical staff to deal with the correspondence. Colonel Ireland Blackburn° thought that Warrington, with the aid of St. Helens and Widnes, would support -the prisoners of their own regiment. People who had adopted prisoners naturally took a special interest in them, and he did not think they would be Willing to send their money to the Central Orgamisation. It was all very well to say they 'would send your name in the parcel. They might or they might not.</p>
        <p>Sir David Gamble said that St. Helens agreed that South Latitaahire ought to look after its own ram', and they . were quite willing to take on Or 300 parcels a week for 200 men, as at esent. They thought the money would be got. it was suggested that Warrington be the centre, assisted by Widnes and St. Miens, and that branches in country district§ should be distributed between these three, so that they might organise and collect funds w4rhin the area and prevent overlapping. He hipped the meeting would 'decide to keep on in the same way. (Applause.) The Mayor of Widnes said they in Widnes were doing their .bit, and were quite prepared to do as much as anybody else., (Hear, hear.) They ought to look after the men of/South- West Lancashire. He would, of course, have to place the information before his committee and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>"THE ONLY LOOPHOLE."</head>
        <p>"THE ONLY LOOPHOLE."</p>
        <p>Local Music-Hall Artiste Deceives Tribunal.</p>
        <p>EXEMPLARY SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>A partner in a pantomime sketch, which it was stated has. been " topping " the .bill at music halls ,for a year, was sentenced to two months' imprisonment by the Warrington magistrates on Saturday for making a false statement whereby the Warrington Tribunal had granted him exemption from military service in June. The prisoner's name is William Briscoe, 31 years of age, and residing in Bank-street,-Warrington. The particular sketch in which he was playing when arrested at St. Helens was not named, but it was stated by Captain MacPhail that Briscoe had been engaged in the profession practically all his life, and was at one time connected with "Wild, Willy and West." Mr. F. H. Swallow, solicitor for the defence, announced that Captain Maenad was only ask- ing for a fine, and he (Mr. Swallow) had advised his client to plead guilty. Briscoe, in the dock, nodded his head in agreement. The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) explained that the maximum penalty for the offence was a fine of .X5O, or six months' im- prisonment with, or without, hard labour. The statement which was sent in by Briscoe was prepared by a solicitor (Mr. Steel) en- tirely on Briscoe's instructions, merely dwelling upon the fact that he had as grocery and green- grocery shop, aild • altogetker suppressing the fact that he was mainly engaged as a music-hall artiste. For the past eighteen months the pris- oner had seldom been off the stage. The Chief Constable added that the Advisory Committee were so impressed that Briscoe was indispensable for the carrying on of the grocery and greengrocery business that-they recom- mended the Tribunal to exempt him. That was done without Briscoe having to appear. Arrested at a Theatre. ,</p>
        <p>Captain MacPhail said that at the time ~f registration, when. there was no question ot conscription of• married men, the prisoner registered as a music hall artiste. When the Military Service Act came into force, however, he wanted to find out some way of keeping out of the Army, and, therefore, sent in an appeal as a grocer, and was, according to the card sent to the military by the Tribunal, exempted on "business and domestic grounds." The general statement did not look at all suspicious, and months had gone by and Briscoe had been free. He would not have been " collared " now, but for the regulation; empowering the military and police to examine the exemption and registra- tion certificates .of men. A few days ago the -police' called at the theatre at which Briscoe was appearing, and in the course of a general examination, noticed the difference between Briscoe's registration card and certificate of exemption, and communicated with him (Captain MacPhail) on the matter. He thought it worth. going into, and on making inquiries saw the statement submitted to the Advisory Committee by the prisoner. The discrepancy was obvious and he had the man arrested. "This man has been advised by somebody who knows a good deal more about the procedure* than he possibly could do," declared the Captain. Mr. Swallow protested, and Captain MacPhail added, "I don't pearl any solicitor. lam making further inquiries into the matter, be- cause he got clear through the only possible loophole he could." The Captain added that if the magistrates imprisoned Briscoe they would give him what he wanted—exemption' from the Army for a time at least. They could fine him heavily, for he had been working at the top of the bill for a long time and earning a high salary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Prisoner's Excuses.</head>
        <p>Prisoner's Excuses.</p>
        <p>In his speech for the defence, Mr. Swallow said that Briscoe undoubtedly carried on a grocery and greengrocery business in Butter- market-street, but it must also be admitted that for the past year he had been in ,partner- ship with two ,other men in a comic pantomime sketch. The reason he did not mention.he was a music hall artiste was that, at the time—in June—there was a very widespread idea that the music halls, or at any rate, a large proportion of them; would have to be and it was his intention to leave the music hall stage entirely. He could, however, not give up the sketch immediately, because if he had done so his two other partners—both married men— would have been thrown out of work. Steps had, however, been taken to fill his place, but when that understudy became competent he had to go into the Army. And so the weeks had gone by .and Briscoe had not been able to get away. Recently, however, a 'man had been found, and Briseoe was soon going to have finished up on the stage of his own accord. Since his arrest the other man had been taking his place in the sketch. The solicitor added that if Briscoe had submitted himself to the Army there was not much doubt that he would have been rejected.</p>
        <p>- The Chairman (Alderman Tinnion): They don't reject anyone to-day. ' In sentencing Briscoe, the Chairman said it was a very serious case. There was no redeeming feature. It was disgraceful that a man should adopt such means in order that other men might fight for him. On Briscoe being removed to the cells his wife screamed and swooned, and his partners carried her out of the Court. A Personal Statement. Mr. T. S. Steel (solicitor), said he wished to make a remark of a personal nature about the case., In preparing the statement no disclosure Was made by the prisoner in regard to his occupation as a music hall artiste. He did not see the man personally, and the particulars were taken by a member of his staff. The Statement was prepared and on the following day the prisoner called and signed it. He (Mr. Steel) did not appear before the Military Tribunal, and he desired to dissociate himself entirely from the whole matter. If anything in regard to the prisoner's profession had been mentioned to him or his staff it would have been incorporated in the statement. He was exceedingly sorry that he had been an innocent instrument in the hands of the prisoner in get- ting the exemption. The Chairman replied that the Bench did not consider him in any way liable, but there had been a little carelessness. It should have been verified.- The Tribunal must be assisted and care should be taken,that a statement-like this was not made again. Mr. Steel said he did not quite see how a solicitor could verify every statement. He had to accept the statement of lis client. Alderman 'Rinnion : This statement is so diverse from the facts of the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>PRISONERS OF WAR.—Continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SALES OF WORK.</head>
        <p>SALES OF WORK.</p>
        <p>£250 RAISED.</p>
        <p>Success Of The Parish Church Sale Of , Work.</p>
        <p>The Parish Church sale of work was brought to a conclusion on Saturday, when there was an- other large gathering of parishioners and friends in the Fairfield Schoolroom. The sale has proved a success from every point of view, the _amount realised on Saturday being £6B, making a grand total of over 0£1.50. • Prior to the opening of the sale, the Rector (the Rev: Canon Willis) addressed the people. He emphasised the importance of a longer period of school education after the war. Re himself was at school and college for a period of six- teen years, from sev-en years of age to twenty- three. Now the average time was from about five years of age to .thirteen—only about half the time. After the war parents should press for more education, both religious and secular. They knew what a serious thing it was when they came•to think of the divisions that existed among Christians. If they were only properly in- structed, 'there could be no divisions at all; they Should all be one in accordance with their Lord's command.</p>
        <p>In conclusion, the Rector spoke appealingly for the.need for contributions towards the organ fund. The organ needed repair and some addi- tions, and the only Means of income towards that fund was the sale of work.</p>
        <p>The students from the Warrington Training College gave a very enjoyable concert in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>COLBORNE STREET BAPTISTS.</head>
        <p>COLBORNE STREET BAPTISTS.</p>
        <p>£123 Realised At Two Days' Sale Of</p>
        <p>Work.</p>
        <p>The opening of the Golborne-street Baptist Church sale of work on Saturday was performed by the children. The Pastor stated that the total takings on Thursday amounted to £9l. _</p>
        <p>The chair was taken by Master Herbert Speakman, who in a neat little speech urged the people to buy, that the Sunday school, which had been built for the children, might be freed from debt.</p>
        <p>The opener was Miss Evelyn Pepper, who seconded the efforts of the Chairman in urging the people to buy and clear the stalls. A vote of thanks was accorded to Miss Pepper, on the motion Of Miss Gladys numizigton, sec- onded by Master Gordon Owen, while a similar vote was moved to the Chairman, on the proposi- tion of Master Frank Lee, seconded by Miss Nellie Lawson.</p>
        <p>During the opening ceremony the children marched round the room, to the accompaniment of Mr. S: Rickers, clitirch cyrganist, and brought their giftu, which were received by the Paster. (the Rev. IL Ogden). The sale throughout the day was successful, and the total takings for the two-days—Thurs- day and Saturday—amounted to £123. All worked so loyally to accomplish this satisfactory result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Fairclough Opens Successful Sale</p>
        <p>Of 'Work.</p>
        <p>To swell the funds of the Latchford Baptist Church and Sunday School, a largely attended sale of work was held in the Schoolroom on Thursday. Mrs. IL Roberts had hoped to per- form the opening ceremony, but, being indis- posed, her daughter, Mrs. Fairclough, came in her stead. With her on the platform were Presided), Mr. T. S. MrsAlbin sjo' Fairhurst vir (whonT. Wilkinson. Mrs. Fairhurst said it gave her great plea- a sure to be there that afternoon, but she was sorry Mrs. Roberts was not present owing to illness. She was pleased to say, however, that she was improving. (Applause.) They were a pleased to see her daughter, Mrs. Fair.. dough, who was doing some very good work in helping the soldiers. (Applause.) Mrs. Fairclongh said it was a great disap- pointment to her mother in not being able to attend, but she had told her to wish the sale every success. In the course of her speech Mrs. Fairclough brought to mind past recol- lections of former years at the Baptist Church, which, she said, she would'always remember. On the proposition of Mr. W. T. Wilkinson, seconded by Mr. T. S. Albinson, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mrs. Fairhurst and the opener. . _</p>
        <p>Several well-laden stalls were in charge of menThers of the church and congregation. The total proceeds for the day amounted to £93 Bs. id.</p>
        <p>The Playful Nurse. Nur.P : "Can anyone here play the bag- pipes ?" Piper McTavish: "Here ye are Miss- -I'm yer man." Nurse: "Oh thank you. Would you mind blowing up this air cushion?"—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>TRAVELLER DISCOURSES.</head>
        <p>TRAVELLER DISCOURSES.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Phythian Commences Lectures On Cqnstantinople.</p>
        <p>The Co-operative Hall was taxed to its ui- most limit on Saturday evening, when the first of a series of lectures on " Constantinope; Its History and Art," was delivered by Mr. J. E. Phythian, whose qualities as a lecturer are always appreciated by Warrington audiences. The lectures will be continued every Saturday until and including the 25th inst. Alderman Bennett presided, anti, 'as a mem- ber of the Museum Committee, said they were anxious that those lectures should be a suc- cess. They felt, as- they must all feel, that the people of this country knew little about other countries and other cities. They had a very limited knowledge of the world, and we could enlarge that knowledge by travelling. We had in 'Warrington the opportunities of going all over the world without travelling, in the walls of the Library. Reading, also, was a good means of improving one's knowledge, but we should read more than the weekly, and daily papers. Names that were little more than names in times past were now very important, and Constantinople was perhaps the most in- teresting of the lot, .(Hear, hear.) _ -</p>
        <p>The Lecturer said the Chairman had raised the question as to whether there was a real Christian city. Somebody, in reply to a state- ment that Christianity had failed, replied: "No, it has never been tried." Christianity, was a great adventure, and it was to be hoped that in the deepest sense of the word it would get a better trial than it had had in the past, or else it would be a poor look-out for our children and grandchildren. Science did not stand still, and wars of the future—if there were any—would be more horrible things than the monstrosity which was now taking place. We should learn from the past. We should all know more about history than we had known in the past, and we should realise what enor- mous moral and spiritual changes had to be made if the world was to go well in future. We ought to know what kind of life made for general human happiness and for peace. We were all blaming one nation, if not one man, for this war. That might be all right in a particular sense, but in a general sense we were all to blame. There were certain kinds of inflammable oils which, having low flash- points, soon broke into flames. What we had been pleased to call peace had had a low flash- point; it had not been worth calling peace. Nations had been trying to be top dog, and that was the breeder of war. Looking over the history of the past one found that com- mercial rivalry had been one Of the most fre- quent causes of war. Such rivalry was not</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>DRINK TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>THE DRINK TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Temperance Reformers Discuss</p>
        <p>" The Trade."</p>
        <p>SHOULD IT BE NATIONALISED?</p>
        <p>Should the drink trade he nationalised?</p>
        <p>This was the subject discussed by well-known gentlemen at a meeting held in the Working Men's Mission oh Monday evening. It originated in an address which Councillor Henry -Roberts delivered in the same place a few weeks ago, in advocaey of nationalisation. On Monday, Mr. W. A. Platt, of Bury, Past High Chief Ruler of the Independent Order of Rechabites, and Past President of the National Cpnference of - Friendly 'Societies, declared vigorously against the proposal, and yearned for prohibition. An interesting dis- cussion followed, Councillor Roberts, Mr. C. E. Parker, the Rev. Irvine Lister, Mr. W, H. Sher- burn and Mr. E. Lloyd Jones being amongst those who took part. Mr. T. Dale presided, and one of his remarks was that through the instance of the Central ,Liquor Control Board, Warrington had benefited. Warrington, he added was a very difficult place to rouse on the drink question, and he thought that all religions bodies in the town had been very remiss in not taking a more prominent part in stamping out the evil. (Applause:) Mr. Platt's speech lacked nothing in vigour, and was vouched in tones evangelistic. De- claring that drink was of the Devil, he said that instead of their going straight to glory by means of prohibition, an attempt was being tried to take total abstainers into the byways by means of nationalisation. What was wanted was the sweeping away of the Trade alto- gether, the removal of temptation from those who could not bear it, and from soldiers' wives. He. was more than a little suspicious of the movement • because brewers and publicans wanted nationalisation. When brewer and temperance reformer got together .nnd went on the same road it was a bad job for the temperance reformer. (Applause.) It was drink that cursed France and caused her to lose the last war, and Russia had been deter- mined to sweep it away. The rising tide of public opinion was with the total abstainer, and Parliament dared not 'bring in a ,Bill to purchase licensed houses in this country. If it were wrong, and he contended it was a sin for a publican or a brewer to sell intoxicants, a% they were undermining thestamina of a nation then in the name of God. it could not be right for the nation to take it on. He '..strongly ttjecied to being a rc.rtner in a giiianti,:! ey (Hear, bear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND THE TRADE'S POWER.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND THE TRADE'S POWER.</p>
        <p>Councill,o- Roberts pointedly prefaced his speech by taking a sip of water from a glass on the table and remarking, "My only bever- age!" With Mr. Platt he favoured total abstinence, but prohibition was not at the pre- sent time practicable. Apparently Mr. Platt was not aware of the enormou,s, power of the Trade in Warrington and all over the country. The reason Russia had been able to throttle the drink trade was because she had nationalised it beforehand; the reason the Government of England did not follow the example of the Czar of Russia and prohibit the sale here was because it was not in the hands of the State: if it had been, niost probably its sale would have been done away with. (Applause.) The 'object of those who favoured nationalisation was not to make money out of it; it was because they realised that if the State got control they could do what they liked. At present with the Trade being in the hands of private individuals —with all classes of, men and women as share- holders—those connected with it were tremen- dously interested in keeping up its sale, and in a thousand ways they all brought to bear upon the Magistracy, Licensing and Watch Cora- -mittees their influence. "Our Trade is .aur politics" was their cry, and never was a more sinistei, one heard. (Hear, hear.) If the Trade ,were nationalised what would happen? He objected to anyone saying that they wanted to do that for the purpose of making money out of it. A great responsibility rested upon the shoulders of those extreme temperance re- formers if they neglected the present state of public opinion to get control of the Trade. Talk about refusing to be partners in the Trade ! They were all partners in it now, by reason of the fact that the nation was taking revenue out of it; and he suggested that if Mr. Platt or anyone else was sincere in his objection he should refuse to enjoy the benefits of Secondary education—which was partly pro- vided by money derived from the. sale of ,whisky -</p>
        <p>—and go to prison for his convictions. (Laugh- ter and applause.) He (Councillor Roberts) was emphatic in condemnation of extreme tem- perance men, for • they • were, not helping. but greatly hindering reform; their influence *upon., public opinion was practically nil. The 'question had not got to be looked at purely from• the sentimental point of view as such as Mr. Platt was doing. They were now parties to the Trade by receiving into the State coffers two-thirds of the profits, and yet they had that without con- trol. Therefore, why not take three-thirds of_ its profits and have control? (Applause.) ,It was said that it was the Devil's business. Per- haps it was, but he would rather have the Devil chained than loose, as he would be if he re- mained unnationalised. Nationalisation was the wily practical step, for public opinion Was. JY Aipe for prohibition. If it were nationalised_ they got rid of the personal interest in the Trade, and the men associated Aith it would be more in favour of temperance than they could possibly be now when their profits de- pended upon the quantity of liquor sold, 'and it was in their own, interests to sell as 'large an amount as they could. (Applause.) Once. they were rid of the personal motive now ruling the sale of intoxicants the men in the .business would be more keen on making England sober. • (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FOR AND AGAINST-MOSTLY FOR.</head>
        <p>FOR AND AGAINST-MOSTLY FOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cottcrill thought the only way to. ,get prohibition .was 'first to -uationalise-the, Trade:. 86did, Mr. E. 'Lloyd Jones, Mr: Wright (who thought that prohibitionists would have to wait too long for their ideas to gain ground and in the meantime they would lose the bene- fit of the reforms which nationalisation would bring about), Mr. Massey and Mr. Heywood. Mr. W. H. Sherburn was against natioludisa- tion, contending that since Russia nationalised it in 1893 its' sale had .increased until it was firmily 'stopped by the edict of the Czar .when war broke out.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts, intervening, thought that</p>
