      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WHERE ARE THE FALLEN?</head>
        <p>WHERE ARE THE FALLEN?</p>
        <p>Rev. T. T. James' Comforting</p>
        <p>Words.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Wycliffe Church on Sunday, the Rev: T. T. James continued his series of sermons on "God and the War." He asked by this question 'whether we are to believe there lies life beyond death. To within a very,ohort time ago the modern emphasis in religion was placed almost entirely upon this life, upon get- ting, things put right here and now, upon the building up of the Kingdom of God upon earth; but he thought we have perhaps been wrong in letting the question of immortality often fall far too much into the background. Now experi- ence comes along and alters everything for us; so that with Vast 'numbers of people at this moment the supreme question is not this life or this world, but the question whether this is the only life and the only world, or whether for those who leave it there is something, else' to follow. When, brave lads are .being buried be- hind the trenches in a storm of shells, and the circle of graN'es is growing in many a strange burying-ground in the villages and in the great cities of France, when the graves are multi- plying of which no- one knows, the' nameless resting places where no flower can be planted and no tear shed, the question rises persistently whether this is the end. - These brave lives, are they finished with, and is thee nothing else to cherish save a memory, or do they continue under. other. conditions, and are we to regard them as transplanted 'to' other spheres? Is it believable that God has already opened the way for them to fuller and more perfect life? He wanted, first of all, to combat the idea that modern thinking and modern knowledge leaves little thorn for the belief in immortality. It is probably true that such a conception is very widely prevalent. We have come to look upon man himself in his origin as related to the rest of the animul creation. It is not surprising that under such conceptions we should ask whether the stupendous claim of immortality can any longer he easily made? Belief in Immortality.</p>
        <p>He thought it was true to say that modern thought is moving now with increasing cer- tainty in the direction of a recognition of the spiritual basis, and such a recognition will place the belief in immortality amongst the things which are reasonable and 'possible.. In the second place for Christian men and women the belief in immortality still rests upon the very fundamentals of the Christian Faith, in the experience of a risen Christ who conquered the power of the, grave. By that he did not mean that it rests merely upon the historical reliability of certain narratives in the New Testament, hut upon the whole history of the Christian Faith. The whole story of the growth and continuity of that faith through nearly two thousand years is inexplicable apart from the belief in a living ,Christ. It has been built upon the conviction that the Founder of that faith is still alive, present with His fol- lowers, a living presence in the world: For Christian men and women the Christ who con- quered death remains the great pledge of im- mortality. "I want to state for my own part," said the preacher, "that I believe with all my heart that there is. life beyond death. Those who have lost may look forward and know that they will find again. Heaven is a reality. I do not think of it as some distant place. If you ask me where it is I do not know that there is any better definition of it than that which the apostle gave. He said that heaven was to be with Christ. 'To me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.' 'We are absent from the body., but present with the Lord: And where is Christ? He is everywhere. He is here. He is here now. And why should not heaven be all about us, and those we love close at hand, invisible enough to our eyes, for they are-living, on a different plane of existence to .which our eyes are not yet open. It does not seem to me that we need imagine it as a place far away. I know sometimes we call it the land which is very far off, but it 'always seems to me to be mistaken poetry; At any rate, early Christian thinking brought it very close. 'Seeing we are compassed about with so -great a cloud of wit- nesses . . . .. let us run with patience the race which is set before us.' The first Chris- tians always wished their dying friends Good- night,' never 'Good-bye'; they wished them ' Good-night ' because they knew they would see them in the morning. We shall see our loved ones in the morning.</p>
        <p>'And with the morn those angel faces smile, Which I have loved long since and lost a while.' "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DRING TRAFFlC.—Continued.</head>
        <p>THE DRING TRAFFlC.—Continued.</p>
        <p>was an argument for himself,, as it was; the fact that Russia had control of the Trade, Which enabled them to crush it when the war hroke. ont. I(Applause.)- , Mr. C. E: Parker also supported •Councilor Roberts'. Mr. Platt, he said, had asked why they should leave the straight road which led to prohibition and glory and enter the byways which led to nationalisation? In reply, he (Mr. Parker) would ask what was the good of re- maining in the straight road if they could not make progress along it? He did not think there was any doubt that if, when war broke out, the Trade had been in the hands of the Govern- ment, Mr. Asquith would have had prohibition. Faced by vested interests, however, the difficul- ties of prohibition were too immense. (Hear, hear.) . In proposing a vote" of thanks to the Chair- man and principal speakers, the Rev. Irvine Lister expressed himself in. favour of national- isation and added 'hat, as this was a democratic country, no one had the, right, by, a stroke of the pen, to. prohibit its sale Unlesathe majority of the 'country were with them.' And, unfor- tunately, at the present time they were not. Mr. plat had given him the impression that he (Mr. Platt) would be more arbitrary than. a Prussian leader and he,:(Mr. Lister) was sure that it was that. spirit Which had hindered all true reforms, especially in the drink trade. (Applause.) If there had not been 'so many extremists they would have .made .greater pro- tests: He advocated nationalisation because it would give the nation a firm hold on the traffic of intoxicating liquor, and having that hold and personal interests, out of the way, they would make more rapid steps-In educating the mthe evil, and in the end the puabslsiecsl%-o regardingulddemand restrictions. (Applauge.) Alderman James Evans seconded, and re- marked that he thought tvtere would be no going back to the old hours of sale, whether the Trade was nationalised or not. Thanks proposition carried. There was 'no vote taken, but the number of speakers favour- ing nationalisation reflected the feeling of..the meeting.</p>
      </div>
