      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>.4.+.c.•:-.:••:••:••:•••••.:••:•4:44—:••.:—).:...:e.,),.:- .:•.:••:...)*•:.•:••:••:••:-:•+•:••:—:••)+4,..:-.:-.:-.:-?</head>
        <p>.4.+.c.•:-.:••:••:••:•••••.:••:•4:44—:••.:—).:...:e.,),.:- .:•.:••:...)*•:.•:••:••:••:-:•+•:••:—:••)+4,..:-.:-.:-.:-?</p>
        <p>H. WOMAN'S WOR D. " "</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Women's Activities.</p>
        <p>The occupations taken up by the three- quarters of 'a million of women who have re- placed men are as varied as those of the most advanced and enterprising of colonial women. Government lumberwomen are cutting down trees in Kewstroke Woods, in Somersetshire, with as much ease and skill as the lumbermen of British Columbia. At the Birmingham Gas_ works women are wheeling barrows loaded with coals to supply the furnaces. In Edinburgh they deliver coals, and lift the sacks as easily as the men who were formerly employed. Women rare driving vans in London and grooming the horses after the day's work. A girl of fifteen has cut fifty tons of hay for the Army, and women in the Yeomanry picket horses, break in vicious mules and horses and clean harness, and do all the stable work. With the woman lamplighter, the 'bus and tram conductor, all are now familiar; also with the girl who calls for grocery orders and de- livers them, and the butcher girl who do*s the same. Under the L.C.C. women and girls are employed as stretcher-bearers, aml4ulance drivers, and attendants; 'whilst one big provi- sion store is run entirely by girls. The Women Patrols.</p>
        <p>The woman of forty, anxious to do something for her country, but debarred from many activities by her age, has the opportunity now of joining the Women's Patrol Service, which is not overdone at present. Miss Dawson' Darner, the chief of the Force, is appealing for helpers. Age is a Matter of health and temperament rather than of years;' many women of forty are stronger and fitter, than their juniors of twenty-five, and that they .are debarred from doing their part at this time is keenly felt by many patriotic, active, and still capable women, considered by the Gov- ernment and employers generally as incapaci- tated by reason of their -years. The duties attached to the Patrol -Service are not too. arduous, and they are mostly performed in the open-air, .whilst the ,pay is fairly good; moreover a pension may be secured in the near future. The 'exercise and drills all help to keep the worker fit. In munition factories the policewomen keeps order, searches the girls for contraband, and engages in patrol work. The towns that have sprung up like mushrooms since war began all require policing, and in this department, at any rate, there is room for many more suitable workers.</p>
        <p>This Season's Modes. Gowns and garments required for those festive occasions that are now the exception rather than the rule are to all appearance quite simple in style. The simplicity is, how- ever, deceptive, for much attention is neces-</p>
        <p>sary to produce this effect. Small details re- ceive serious consideration at the hands of the expert that would be overlooked entirely by the inexperienced hands, and success is the result. Gowns, too, must be beautifully cut to produce the novel lines orfullness that give them their note of distinction. The chief features of this season's fashions in dress, as compared with those of the summer and spring, are a straighter outline, and longer sleeves. Skirts are longer, too, of that there can be no doubt; and the short of stature should rejoice that La Mode has for once been kind to them. A very lovely evening gown of the newest type was a modified Prin- cess, and the skirt was short at the sides, longer in front, and had a square train at the back. Gowns made of heavy materials fit over the hips, but are quite full at the feet; whilst those of diaphanous fabrics are still full from the waist. Yorkshire Dinner Rolls.</p>
        <p>Ingredients, 11b. of flotir, 2oz. of lard or butter, ,ioz. of yeast; 1 teaspoonful of salt, 1 lump of sugar, 1 pint of milk. Method: Rub the flour through a sieve into a basin, and make a well in the centre. Melt the fat in a saucepan; add the sugar and milk, and heat them 'until lukewarm. Cream the yeast with the salt, pour the milk, &amp;c., over it, and'strain all into the middle of the flour. Work the latter gradually into the liquid, and then beat the dough with the hand for about twenty minutes, until it looks light and full of air. Now cover the basin and put it in a warm place to allow the dough to rise. When well risen work in more flour to prevent it stick- ing; make up into small rolls, and place them on a greased tin. Let them stand to rise for a few minutes, and then bake in a good oven. Brush over with a little milk a short time before they finish cooking.—" Guardian."</p>
        <p>Leman Jelly Cake. Cream a cupful of butter and two of sugar totether, add three cupfuls of sifted flout with two teaspoonfuls of baking-powder; mix, pour in half a teacupful of sweet milk, and lastly, add the well-beaten whites of ten eggs and a teaspoonful of almond extract. Bake in jelly- tins. For filling, take one cupful of sugar, a tablespoonful of butter, the yolks of two eggs and the juice and grated rind of one lemon; put in a small saucepan and cook until thick; let cool and put between the cakes.</p>
        <p>Preventing Boils. First of all, it must be known that boils are due to germs that are constantly present on the surface of the skin. These germs are capable of producing small abscesses or boils if by any means they work their way beneath the akin. Those surfaces which are most subject to boils should be washed with soap and water two or three times a day, then washing with a weak solution of iodine. As a preventive or bons, clean blood is essential. When the blood be- comes filled with impurities as a result of in- activity of the bowels, bails are likely to make their appearance because of the diminished re- sistance of the body cells.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>• • Children' 8 Corner • .. • • • Conducted by UNCLE JACK. • -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CHRISTMAS IN THE TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS IN THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— I have derived a great deal of pleasure from reading your ideas of how Christmas should be spent at the Front. There is very little doubt that if you had your way "Tommy" would be very well off on the occasion of the great 'festival. I only wish I could reproduce all your letters, but you will see from the ex- tracts I have printed what your cousins think. I have given two prizes, one to a nephew at Hamilton-street School and the other to a niece at Evelyn-street School. Their names are</p>
        <p>field School):—" I think if they could-do with- out fighting for, Christmas Day they would enjoy it much better, as it is a day of peace. I hope they alt get some nice big letters and parcels from home, ,or at least all the Warring- ton lads, as I expect my brother= will. The Christmas Pudding Fund in 'The Examiner' is a grand idea, and I, am sending a shilling to- wards it which I have collected selling lavender bags."</p>
        <p>Niece Amy Campbell, ,56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Peace and good-will should reign over all the world and fighting should cease at least on this special day. The soldiers who are at liberty at the Front might join in singing the good old-fashioned Christ- mas hymns. kj?epreation and concerts and amusements of different kinds might be arranged."</p>
        <p>Nephew Colin Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street School).—" In the afternoon they can have a game of football and a smoke to corpfort them at half-time. In the evening they can play cards and have a chat about what they did before the war."</p>
        <p>Niece Ada Shipley, 67, Cartwright-strect (Evelyn-street School).—" Christmas Day should be a day of rest for the men at the Front if it is possible. It would be nice if they could have Church service in the morning and then their dinner and plum puddings. The rest of the Clay could be spent resting and opening the parcels from home. I think they, would be pleased to spend their day in•thi&amp;may." Niece Emmeline Spencer, 58, Winwick-road, does not think it would be nice to have a meal in trenches whilst shells were bursting. Nephew Wm. Bibby, 21, Scott-street (Hamil- ton-street School) says:—" Let the Men enjoy themselves the best way they can ;or try to mak'd a great lull, ano if that is impossible I would let some of the Men fight in the morning while the others are enjoying themielves, while those who fought in the morning should enjoy them- selves in the afternoon while the others fought"</p>
        <p>Nephew Ernest Blackledge, 111, Foster- street (Hamilton-street School).—" Our soldiers will be looking out for parcels from their hones. They will be wanting mouth-organs to cheer them up when they are not fighting. If one soldier has a parcel and another one has not, he should divide it among his pals• and have a little sport. The Christmas puddings the town has sent will make them think they are having Christmas at home." Niece Florrie Overend, 74, Lythgoes-lane.---- " Send them Christmas cards and mince pies (besides the plum puddings) to make them feel as comfortable as if they were at home. We could send them mince pies and other kind of Christmas cakes so that they will feel happy. Niece Jane Gough, 5, Phillip-street.—" We can send them woollen helmets and mittens to keep them warm in the snow; and Christmas pud- dings and cakes to share them with each other." Niece Eva M. Morrall says the Huns could not be trusted to keep et truce; but she would like all our soldiers to have an abundance of good things to remind them. of .home, prayers to be offered up for the success of, our just cause.</p>
        <p>Nephew Geo. H. Bradshaw, 85, Birchen- street.—" Every British 'Tommy' should have, if possible, a goat-skin coat and be provided with a Christmas pudding. If any man has been on active service more than twelve months and not had a furlough let him come and spend Christmas with his wife and children." Nephew Harold Britton, 69, Birchall-street.---- " I think books might be sent to them contain- ing Christmas Carols. The officers should try to make the men cheerful by getting mouth- organs, violins, concertinas and tin whistles. The men should keep changing sentries, so that each man might have his share of the fun." Nephew Thomas Atherton, 50, Scott-street.— "If just for such a short time as twenty-four hours the British and German foes could mix and talk together quite freely it would, under the circumstances, make CNristmas Day seem more homelike. Christmas carols could be sung but not national antheins, for fear of arousing national hatred between the British and Ger- man foes."</p>
        <p>Nephew Percy Gregory, 105, Orford-lane (Hamilton-street Scheol).—" No anthems should be sung, but carols, for fear of any attack by the Germans when they hear the phrase God save the King' of England, and they will start strafing the British Tommies.' No artillery duels should be fought, but chaplains anfi priests should give a service on the birth of our Lord esus Christ."</p>
        <p>Nephew Tom Skidle, 70, Battersby-lane.—"On the eve of the great day I think that every soldier will think- of the Mother Country. After parcels have becn opened and shared everyone will think of and praise God for giving his Son to prevent the world from being wicked. Games will be enjoyed." • Nephew Harold Freaney, 107. Cartwright- street (Sacred Heart Sell6o1).—" The soldiers instead of ringing a bell at 12 o'clock on Christmas Eve should fire three large shells so the soldiers would know what was coming." Nephew James Hill, 58, Chorley-street roet School), favours an armistice.</p>
        <p>Alban's School), thinks the members of the Children's Corner should collect to send the soldiers and, sailors mouth-organs and music?' • Nephew Wm. Watkinson, 7, Heaton-street (llamilton-street School), says our soldiers should have such a feed on Christmas Day as they had never before seen; priests and mission- aries should be sent out, so as to keep our Lord's name and birth well-known. Some of the men should have a short furlough behind the trench4s. Books and papers should \be sent; field concerts could be held 'in order to cheer up our gallant men with iron hearts.</p>
        <p>Nephew Fred Rathbone, 112, Algernon-street (Parochial School).—" The Tommies ' will be singing all sorts of songs if they are at liberty." Nephew Fred Smith, 81, Algernon-street (Hamilton-street School).—" I hope all the men will get lots of goodies and cheery letters from home, especially the Warrington lads, as I know my soldier-brother, who is out in France, will, I hope they will have a football match and enjoy it very much. I think The Examiner' Pudding Fund a splendid idea, and I hope all the Warrington lads get one of your puddings, and also a copy of The Examiner,' which lam sure they will enjoy, very much." • Niece ~DOriti - Whittaker, 62, Baxter-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Some soldiers may get parcels sent to- them by their friends, and they may share' them with their chums or some may have a little concert with themselves while they are not having heavy fig-r.tting." Nephew H. Heywood, 8, Matthews-street (Parochial School), says the day should begin with piayer, asking God to keep them safe. Then football, and after dinner the men should enjoy themselves telling " riddles " and their adventures. They would read tie cards from home and sing songs. Niece Nancy Adams, 27, Samuel-street.— "Our soldiers should have warm clothes, for it is no use them trying to fight for us half- starved with the biting frost; also nice hot strengthening drinks and food. They might, I think, have all they need on Christmas Day, including pipes and tobacco." •</p>
        <p>Niece Maud Lunny, 1(i8a, Bridge-street (Arpley-street School).—" It would be a great pleasure if every person should send them eaclv a small parcel to cheer them up. Poor neople who cannot afford to do so much can send some money to 'The Examiner Office." Niece Lizzie Lewis, 21, Samuel-street.—" I am saving up my coppers towards buying cigarettes for our soldiers. They like a smoke if they don't get anything else. so 'We all ought to try and give them a good time, as we will be having one ourselves."</p>
        <p>Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick-road, thinks the soldiers should have luxuries and should have a bright and merry time. They are fighting for our sake, so we should do some- thing for their sake." Nephew Denis Farrar, 46, Willis-street (Parochial School),, says English and German should let the large whiz-bangs rest and settle in a clean part of the trenches and have a nice concert and a feast, the Governments of Eng- land and Germany providing the necessary money. They might play at bayoneting rats, and if there was ice they might have a jolly time on No Man's Land. They should have a wash at night and be ready for the fighting the fol- lowing morning. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>1047 Nephew Frank Holmes,</head>
        <p>1047 Nephew Frank Holmes,</p>
        <p>1, Hamilton-street; age 12; Hamilton-street School; and Niece Lily Williamson, 53, Priestley-street; age 12; Evelyn-street I have not got Niece Lily's number. Perhaps. next week she will supply me with the informa- tion.</p>
