      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>I have had a note from Nephew Prank Overend asking me how many members of Par- liament there are. In England there are two Houses of Parliament, the House of Lords and the House of Codimons. The number of mem- bers in the House of Lords is always changing as members die, sometimes without leaving suc- cessors, and the King makes new members when he wishes to reward distinguished services. At the end of last year the total was 654. The House of Commons has 670 members, who are elected by stated constituencies. I am always very pleased to be able to help my young friends by answering their questions. You will notice that the Corner is growing week by week. I wish all my new nephews and nieces would tell me all about themselves so nicely as Nephew John Kent does this week. Here is his letter:— "7, Edward-street, Nbvember 9th, 1916. "Dear Uncle -Jack,—l, am so anxious to join the Corner. Every time, I get hold of 'The Examiner I find new members every week. I know some of the boys and girls who have won prizes. I know the Corner is getting bigger, and I will make it bigger.—Yours truly, JoHN KENT, age 12; Silver-street School." A new member, Herbert Dixon, Grappenhall, sends a neat and complete application, along with a fine sketch of the Connemara. He will evidently do well when we have a drawing com- petition.</p>
        <p>- Many forget their full name, age or school, but John did not.</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE SUCCESSFUL LETTERS.</p>
        <p>By Nepliew Frank Holmes, 1, Hamilton- street :</p>
        <p>"On Christmas Day an armistice could be given and the soldiers could have a service con- ducted by the.chaplain_ of the regiment. They could make their dug-outs into a very large one and then all of them could go in it and make themselves homelike. Some of them could sing carols and songs. They could have their Christmas puddings which 'The Examiner' is sending them. While no bullets will be flying the Tommics ' can have a game of football. which I expect -would be welcomed by some of them. They would, however, have a better time than we will, and so they ought to, because they are upholding the Empire. I enclose my Saturday earnings. for Christmas puddings for the gallant sons of the Empire." By Niece Lily Williamson, 53, Priestley- street :</p>
        <p>"I have often thought how do our soldiers spend Christmas Day at the Front. I know that they do not spend it like they did in pre- vious years, or like we do, but they could try, for it is not right that we should be happy while our soldiers are giving their lives for us. On Christmas Day I should like them to stop fighting and to put up a truce flag and' to be merry/just as if they were at home, not to be downhearted. Perhaps their parents could send a mouth-organ and a music book, with which they could have a concert. Theft parents could send them some cakes, &amp;c., and they would be merry if they had a fire, for I know that it is very cold at Christmas time. I am sure that on Christmas Day our sbldie'rs will be thinking of earlier years, when they were having fun with their family, but I sup- pose that they will do the same if they are spared when this great strife is ended." "Gladys Morris and I are making dolls' clothes and selling them just to our friends. As soon as we receive all the money we will send it: to you for Christmas puddings:"</p>
      </div>
