      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>POTATO PRICES.</p>
        <p>Are Farmers Acting The Part</p>
        <p>Of Pro-Germans?</p>
        <p>Mr. Powell, the president, speaking at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Fruiterers' Association, on Monday, referred to the pre- sent excessive price of potatoes. In 1913 and 1914, the average price of potatoes, he said, was £2 10s. to £3 los. per ton wholesale. The price was in 1915 £3 10s. to 2.1 10s.; and in 1916 the price had been from £9 to '2ll. The approxi- mate average cost in pre-war days was £l5 to £lB per acie, and the present day cost £lB to £2O per acre. This year's late crop of potatoes had not been as successful as in the corresponding period of previous years, though, in his opinion, the earlier crop of this year was anything from 20 per cent. to 30 per cent. above the normal standard. Consequently it is very diffididt to understand the exception- ally high prices of potatoes, especially consider- ing they are not an article that can be kept in good condition for any length of time, and also the extra cost in labour for hogging :potatoes, particularly having regard to the un- favourable atmospherical conditions we are at present experiencing. It behoved all members of the Association to work together in meeting, and, if possible, resisting the present trouble. He was surprised that the market was not glutted. He .desired, as President, to record a most emphatic protest against the prevailing high prices, which, to his mind, were out of all reason. The general public were protesting against these high prices, but, unfortunately, the blame was placed upon the retailer, who, it was as well to make clear, was not the per- son in the least responsible. It was difficult to understand what qualifications outside people possessed to enable them to write articles to the Press and to offer expert opinion as to who was responsible for the rise, especially when they directed both these facts against the retailer. There would be little room to complain, having regard to the increased cost of labour, manure and other incidental charges, if the rise in the price of potatoes had beeil about 25 per cent., but , a rise of 200 per cent. was positively pre- posterous. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Moss stated for the benefit of the public at large that on Saturday the approximate price of potatoes for . wholesale purchase was 9s. to us., according to quality. The price at which these potatoes were sold throughout the town averaged 51b. for 7d.;, the fruiterer received 12s. per cwt., to say nothing of six or seven pounds of soil which found its way into each hamper. This would show for itself the mean profit which fell into the pockets of the retailer.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harter, vice-president, who brought to the attention of the meeting the fact that the President of the Board of Trade had published a notice to, the effect that names and addresses of farmers were requested, against whom it could be proved that they were holding up potatoes. It was further stated unless there was a very quick, and favourable improvement he, for one, would ,supply the names and addresses of certain farmers, who he knew for a fact were holding up potatoes and thereby indulging in the action which we would only expect of a pro-German.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>,"7INFER'S PIE, 1916."</head>
        <p>,"7INFER'S PIE, 1916."</p>
        <p>We are glad to hear that that ever-welcome annual, "Winter's Pie," will .be published on Monday, the lath inst.; and, judging by the following list of eminent authors and artists who have contributed to its pages, it will un- doubtedly come as a cheery gleam to those on active service, in hospitals and camps, or at home.</p>
        <p>There are stories by A. S. Barrow, G. B. Burgin, Dion Clayton Calthrop, Lieut.-Colonel Newnham-Davis, Hamilton Fyfe, Harry Gra- ham, Keble Howard, Arthur Lambton, William Le Queux, Sir Henry Lucy, Barry Pain, Max Pemberton, Mostyn T. Pigott, W. Pett Ridge, George R. Sims, J. D. Symon, and Edgar Wal- lace. Drawings are contributed by a great array of well-known artists.</p>
        <p>"Winter's Pie" is published by the Sphere aid Tatler, Ltd., and obtainable at all Book- sellers and Bookstalls, Is. net.</p>
      </div>
