      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PRICES.</head>
        <p>PRICES.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1916. THE FOURTH TIME.</p>
        <p>Alderman Peacock Advocates Permanent War Memorial.</p>
        <p>CURE FOR PROFITEERING.</p>
        <p>Amid the praise of Aldermen and Town Councillors, and the approval of burgesses of the borough assembled in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, on Thursday, at noon, Alderman Peter Peacock was elected Mayor of Warrington for the fourth year in succession. This is, a local record'. The ceremony was not without the pomp becoming the honour and dignity of the occa- sion, the Mayor being conducted to his chair by Sergeant Leonard and P.C. Plumb, who were clothed in mantles of gold• and royal purple, and carried the gold -mace .and sword respectively. The Mayoress, wearing her gold chain of office, was present, sitting with her lady friends. In accordance with custom, Alderman Pea- cock retired from the Chamber while the Council considered and passed the mayoral resolution, under the chairmanship of the Deputy-Mayor.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst moved and Alderman Tinnion seconded the Mayor's re-election. Sup. porting, Councillor Pemberton, in the course of his remarks, said he did not know whether the public fully recognised the monetary sacrifices the Mayor made to become Mayor of War- rington. It was questionable whether anyone else would have done what he did, but he did it cheerfully in the interests of freedom and fair play, and those who supported and fought hard for him had since been highly gratified by the enormous success which had followed him. Warrington should be proud to honour such a gentleman; (Applause.) Liberal, he was indiscriminating In his gifts.. He was a good steward ed his wealth. (Applause.) There were thousands of men who were worth a hundred times more than he yet who did' not do a fiftieth part of the good he did. (Ap- plause.) Alderman Bennett added, a tribute, as also did the. Deputy-Mayor, before putting the resolution, which was carried with general applause. PRACTICAL MEMORIAL TO OUR LOCAL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HEROES.</head>
        <p>HEROES.</p>
        <p>The Mayor's speech in acknowledgment was happily phrased and full of ,hope for the future. After thanking the Council. for the honour they had done him in Conferring the Mayoralty upon him for the fourth year in succession he said : Warrington 'as a town has lost many of its brave sons; their names will be honoured amongst us for all time, and I hope that we shall have some permanent memorial in Warrington to carry down through the ages the never-to-be- forgotten story of their bravery—(applause)-- something more dignified than a mural tablet, and more useful than a statue. I, for one, have no love for that peculiar form of culture, which signalises its being by the affixing of metal placques or terracotta lozenges to the walls of our town. (Laughter, and hear, hear.) What better memorial to our lads could there be than the provision of some permanent institution— say, perhaps, a Warrington home for disabled soldiers? Perhaps the idea thrown out to-day may in the future mature, for with the experi- ,ence that I have gained during the last two years of Warrington's generosity when a worthy object is concerned, I am sure ,there smms little doubt of success. . . • Work for the Army.</p>
        <p>The year has also witnessed the remarkable event of Universal Military Service, preceded in the November and December of last year by the wonderful voluntary effort, known as the Derby Scheme. I should like to take this opportunity of thanking all those who in Warrington did so much to make that scheme a success. Those of us who recall the abnormal rush to the Baths will always think well of the patriotism of the town. Arising ont of the Derby Scheme and the Military Service Acts, we could not pass without comment the work of the Tribunal, so ably pre- sided over by my deputy (Dr. Joseph). (Ap- plause.) Knowing, as I -do, the extremely diffi- cult and thankless task they have had to per- form of holding the Scales of justice evenly between the, citizen and the State, and the number of cases that they have had before them, I feel that I am voicing the common view when I say that they have done their duty without fear or favour, and in a Manner which has earned the respect of all. (Hear, hear.) I doubt, if we searched the whole of. England through, whether we should come across a body of men who gave so much care and' attention to the case of every claimant. Illear, hear.) The year also has witnessed the passing of the good work, so ably done by the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association, with their home at Parr Hall. (Hear, hear,) A new body called the War Pensions Statutory Committee had been</p>
        <p>_ set up. Whether its complicated machinery will be any better than the simple, but efficient; methods we had at work before remains to be seen, but a great debt of gratitude is due to the band of ladies who worked so ably and so well for two years. (Hear, hear.) Without their help and the splendid work of Mr. Spinks and Mr. Houghton, the dependants of Warrington would have had a great cause of complaint. (Applause.) There are many other changes since the last 9th of November.</p>
        <p>Novel Kinds of Blessings.</p>
        <p>We have become accustomed to many things to which we were all unaccustomed—the early closing of shops, the restriction of licensing hours, flag days, the darkened streets, and other similar blessings—(laughter)—which the exigen- cies of the times force upon us. Despite, how- lever, these inroads upon our ideas of indi- vidual freedom, I do think that as a nation we shall emerge from this conflict as a better race, with greater ideas of patriotism, and the put- ting of the State first and above all. (Applause.) I almost forgot in mention another innovation during the, year. As it is a.domestic one, I do not know whether it is qilite in place here, and that is the birth of my war baby—(applause)— whose entrance into the world was marked by the Council and officials by the very handsome presentation the other day. Thanks for Support.</p>
        <p>My grateful thanks are due to you, gentlemen, for the very kindly way in which you have helped me during the year, and for the very ,happy way in which the business of the town has been conducted. To the public generally I owe a deep debt of gratitude for the, splendid way in which they have supported every fund which has been raised during the year. My special thanks are. due to. the officials of the Council (particularly to Mr. Hallaway), who, working with depleted staffs (every available single man„ having 'joined . the Colours), have rendered invaluable assistance to the and the borough, 'asSistance Which has been' given whole- heartedly and willingly. To the Press also I extend my very sincere thanks. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Hard Work Before Dark 'Clouds Go.</head>
        <p>Hard Work Before Dark 'Clouds Go.</p>
        <p>I may say that there. is. a great deal of hard work before our legislators and our Selves... The increasing prices of all commodities demand some urgent and instant action upon. the part of the Government. At times of national peril no section of the community should make undue profit out of its fellow men. (Hear, hear.) Other. difficult situations have been ably handled, and I 'trust that drastic steps will be taken with all "profiteers." Universal, con- scription of all the resources of the country seems to. be the only remedy, .an unprecedented step perhaps, but we are living in unprecedented times. (Hear, hear.) , . • I could not conclude, gentlemen, without ex- pressing the hope that before another year has gone the dark clouds of war may have been swept from the horizon and the glorious sun- shine of a lasting and righteous peace be once more spread over our land. (Applause.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Miss Lucy Broadbent in Rumania.</head>
        <p>Miss Lucy Broadbent in Rumania.</p>
        <p>Miss Lucy Broadbent, of The Hollis, Latch- ford, who is with the Scottish women's hospital unit, has arrived in Rumania after an interest- ing journey from Archangel to Odessa. Every- where the party were received with enthusiasm. The last letter, dated October 9th, from Miss Broadbent, was from Medjidie, which the Ger- mans have since occupied. Consequently, anxiety was felt as to the safety of the party, but a telegram has just been received that the hospital is now at Galatz, north of the Danube, and that the members are safe and very busy. It is the only hospital unit with the Serbian division of the Russian Army, and an urgent appeal has been received from Dr. Elsie Inglis for hospital requisites. The whole of the,hos- pital is worked by women, including orderlies, and ambulance drivers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN PER PEACOCK Was elected Mayor, on Thursday for the fourth tinie in succession, and thereby set up a record which will not be easily or rapidly overtaken. His WORSHIP has given liberally of his wealth, as wealth is understood to mean money; but, as ALDERMAN TINNION said, it is the duty of those who have money to give as far as it is in their power to do so. There is, as the ALDERMAN bluntly, stated, very little virtue in giving away money if You possess it. There are those, it is true, who have it and stick to- it, war or no war, as if their salvation depended upon the size of their bank balance; but theirs is the shame. What is meritorious it hat the MAYOR has given of himself—he has been un- wearying in giving his time, his services and his ability for the welfare of the people of the town, and especially the afflicted portion of the people of the town. We hope, as he and those who placed him in the high office once again hoped, that before his successor is ap- pointed he will have had the added 4lnty and pleasure of managing the town's demonstra- tions that will celebrate the conclusion of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>MASONIC INSTALLATION.</head>
        <p>MASONIC INSTALLATION.</p>
        <p>Bro. John Upson Master Of " Lodge Of Friendship."</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance of provincial and local Masons at the Masonic Hall on Monday night on the occasion of the installa- tion of Bro. John. Upson as W. 3.1: of the "Lodge of Friendship'," 2963: Brethren in the teaching profession were present in considerable num- •bers. The installing, master was W.Bro. J. Al. Murray, I.P.M. Upon his appointment the new Master appointed his officers as follows Bro. C. G.: Stone; J.W., Bro. S. Wallhead ; Chap- lain, W.Brb: H. V. Pigot, Chaplain Grand Lodge of England; Treasurer, W.Bro. J. M.. Murray, i.P.M.; Secretary, W.Bro. H. H. Evans, P.M.; D.C., W.Bro. Greenhouse; S.D., Bro. J. Birtles ; J.D., Bro. J. Clarke; Organist, Bro. F. Webster; 1.G., Bro. H. B. Monk; Stewards, 'Bros, B. N- Silcock, H. H. Taylor, J. T. Cooper, A. 0. Hilditch.</p>
        <p>The visitors included W.Bros. D. Tinnion, P.M., P.Pr.G.D., 2651; P. Robinson, 1.P.M., 758; W.ll. Hughes, 1.P.M., 1345; W. H. Stuns.. field, P 31., D.C., 2651; H. Woods, 1.P.M., 3597; H. E. Travis, P.M., 3597; F. Rose, P.M., 1250; F. B. Goodacre, P.M., Pr. Assist. G.S.; J. Critchley, P.M:, 148; J. Evans, P.M.. 148; H. F. Greenough, P.M., P.Pr.G.S.B., 3597; T. I. Fletcher, WM., 148; F. C. J. Dyke, P.M., P.S.G.D.; W. Mason, P.M., P.Pr.G.D.C., 3060; S. Pickton, W.M., 3597; J. W. Hey- wood, P.M., 3287; H. Taylor, W.M., 1250; J. S. Hill, W.M., 2651; and Bros. J. Wright, 2651; A. Lowe, 148; A. S. Elliott, 2651; I. Bowen, 148; T. B. Handley, 3597; Rev. E. Downham,, Chaplain, 3597; J. T. Cragg, 295; J. W. Parker, 2651; F. Hollingworth, S.W.; 2651; J. S. Broome, 1052; A. Knowles, 148; W. Loxham, 3597; F. Lowe, 2651; F. B. Holt, 2651; J. H. Teare, A.S., 3597; T. Locker, 148; J. Banks, 3.D., 148; T. F. Matthews, See., 3597; J. Cann. 2651; F. Whitelegg, 1250; D. E. Wat- son, 2826.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CHRISTMAS PUDDINGS FOR "TOMMY."</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS PUDDINGS FOR "TOMMY."</p>
        <p>Fund Nearly Sufficient For Two</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Battalions.</head>
        <p>Battalions.</p>
        <p>"The Examiner" Fund, in aid of the Army Christmas Pudding Fund, the only medium through which Plum Puddings may be sent to our men at the Front this year, now stands at nearly ..f.37. That is nearly enough for two Battalions, and about one-half the amount Warrington should contribute, if Warrington is to do its duty. This week's contributions include £3 3s. from the Atkinson Cafes, Ltd., per Mr. J. Wilkin- son, the secretary, and sixpence from a member of our Uncle Jack's Children's Corner. All items, big and little, are welcome; and as time is now pressing we hope those who are intend- ing to give will do their kindly act as soon as possible. Remember that you could not send the smallest parcel to the Front for less than is. in postage alone. That shilling would pay for two puddings under our scheme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
