        <p>• We liould like each of our readers to be the host of at least one soldier on Christmas Day. Next Saturday there will be a collection for the fund on the Warrington Football Ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>The Mayor ended his third year of office badly. Entering the Council Chamber on Thursday he greeted his colleagues as follows Our first business to-day is the election of Mayor: In accordance with custom I will leave the chair.</p>
        <p>It is not the leaving of the chair to which I object; it would, in fact, have been indecorous had tie Mayor remained to preside over his own re-election. What I regret is that he lapsed into verse. I have it on the authority of a Secondary School Miss that poetry, is only a habit.</p>
        <p>If this is correct I hope his Worship will wean himself before the habit has got a firm hold. We have one Alderman full of wise verses and a Councillor full of telling quota- tions, in both verse and prose.</p>
        <p>But let us save the Mayoral Chair. It would be especially unfortunate if anything went wrong Lwith the Mayor now—he is our only possiale Mayor for the duration of the war. I am told by the Editor that everything that is said about his Worship's generosity is literally true. When "The Examiner" opened the Army Christmas Pudding Fund his Wdrship called and guaranteed the money to feed a battalion, £2l, but, fortunately, the readers of the paper responded so readily that the guaran- tee was not needed.</p>
        <p>That was an interesting discussion .at the Working Men's Mission on Monday night, on the Nationalisation of the Drink Trade. One or two speakers thought that the nation should have nothing to do with it, their opinions being that they should not trade with the Devil " as one prohibitionist put it. Eyidently to them a case of "Trading. with the Enemy" !</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts, who advocates nationali- sation, mentioned some interesting facts not cited in our report of the discussion. Since Mr.</p>
        <p>Balfour's Licensing Act came into operation, he pointed out, the maximum compensation had been levied on public-houses in the borough. £20;000 had been paid in eleven years and only eleven licences have been extinguished—one licence per annum. -</p>
        <p>There are still 170 odd licensed houses in the borough, which works out at one for every 450 of the inhabitants, and, deducting teetotalers and children, this means one house for about every 100 to 150 adult people. At the present rate of extinguishing licences it will take 100 years to reduce 'them to one per thousand, which "is considered by experts to be a reason. able and proper proportion."</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Stewart Royston, who has done so much to provide wounded soldiers with all possible ease by means of his walking-sticks, which he has distributed far and wide, has received his iron cross.</p>
        <p>The Cross comes only indirectly from the Kaiser- That is to say, it is made of aluminium from' one of the Zeppelins we wrecked recently near London. It was roughly cut and was sent to Mr. Royston by a friend whose father was on the spot at the time. We hope the Kaiser will pardon these familiarities being taken with his property.</p>
        <p>Newspaper placards when left to do duty too long become very misleading. "Another 1.000 prisoners captured" creates a very comforting feeling until one finds that one has already gloated over that thousand a week ago.</p>
        <p>Sometimes the wear sand tear of time—per- haps with the assistance of some street urchin —improves, the "news." I was startled the other day to see, an "Echo" placard with the legend</p>
        <p>Mackenson oiled.</p>
        <p>As I had. always been led to suppose that the oil treatment was to be reserved for the Kaiser, Z inquired. further, only to find that the weather, or the, urchin. had removed an " f " frOm before. the "oiled."</p>
        <p>Warning to lighting offenders: The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) said at Thursday's sitting at the, Borough Police Court that the Bench were considering yhether it was not expedient that heavier fines should be inflicted, even 'in: domestic cases. So, as Carmen sings "Beware!" VIGILANT.</p>
        <p>• The Kinemas. "A Woman's Law," featuring Florence Read, is a fine picture now show,itig at the Star Kinema House, Church-street. During the first half of next week we are to have "His Picture in the Papers," and for the latter half "The Moon- stone" from the famous novel by Wilkie Col- lins, while "The King with hisiadvanced Army at the Somme," a special picture, will also be screened. The last-nained is a remarkable his- toric film.</p>
        <p>At the Grand Electric Theatre, Bridge Foot, "Tier Great Price" is now being 'shown. -Next week Lina Cavalieri, the world-famed prima donna and actress, will be seen in "Should a Man Forgive?" and the twenty-third episode of "The Diamond from the Sky" for the first half, while for the second half "The Explorer," featuring Lou Tellegen, and the seventh episode of "Peg o' the Ring" are booked.' "Abide with me," a dramatic film, now holds sway at the Pavilion, Sankey Green. "His Neighbour's Wife" is the tit] of the most prominent picture for the first half of next week, and there will also be shown the twenty-fourth episode of " ThA Diamond from the Sky." The star pictures for the latter half are "The Golden Pavement" and the seventh episode of "Peg o' the Ring."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>1 L"144 V.iti CAL 1:1',-! TEL LIGE %co.,</head>
        <p>1 L"144 V.iti CAL 1:1',-! TEL LIGE %co.,</p>
        <p>Lights For Coming Week. Light tip Obscure Vehicles. Lights. Saturday Sunday Monday Tuesday Wednesday Thursday Friday Saturday</p>
        <p>Evangelistic Services .at Gospel Hall.</p>
        <p>On Siinday and week nights from November 12th to 25th, Mr. W. MacFarlane, of Exeter, is to conduct special evangelistic services at the Gospel Hall, Forster-street.</p>
        <p>Temperance Sunday. To-morrow being Temperance Sunday, upublic meeting will be held in the Co-operative Hall in the evening. Mrs. James Dodds, of Penketh, will preside, and the speakers will be theßev. A. Leathley Heap, of Leeds, and the Rev. Irvine Lister.</p>
        <p>Royal Theatre of Varieties Put UT) For</p>
        <p>Auction.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, November 22nd, Mr. Herbert Johnson will sell by auction at the Blue nen Horsemarket-street, the Royal Theatre of Varieties, Scotland-road.</p>
        <p>£2l For Dr. Barnardo's Homes. The recent envelope collection in Warrington for Dr. Barnarsdo's Homes realised £2l.</p>
        <p>Friends' _Meeting House.</p>
        <p>At the Friends' Meeting flo—use, Battermarket- street, to=morrow evening, Mr. R. J. Long, of Leads, will speak on "The overcoming of fear."</p>
        <p>Bread Still Dearer.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Master Bakers' Association announce a further advance in the price of bread of one penny a 41b. loaf from Monday next.</p>
        <p>Wallington Market Prices. At Wednesday's Warrington Market wheat sold at 9e. 9d. to IGs. per 701 b.; oats, ss. 3d. to ss. 4d. per 451 b.; flour, 495. to 525. per 2401 b.; thirds, 2s. 9d. to 2s. 11d. per 201 b.; bran, 2s. ld. to 2s. 3d. per 20113. The Mayor at Church.</p>
        <p>To-morrow-1;einillo;;s Sunday, the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) will attend the Parish Church in the morning, being accom- panied by members of the Corporation and the leading townspeople. In the evening he will at- tend Golborne-,street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>British and Foreign Bible Society. The annual meeting of the Warrington Auxiliary of the British and Foreign Bible Society will be held on Thursday evening next in the Co-operative Hall. The Rev. Arthur Taylor, of London, secretary of the British and Foreign Bible Society, will give an address. A Testimonial from a Wood's College</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>"Open Temperance Meetintj.</head>
        <p>"Open Temperance Meetintj.</p>
        <p>An open temperance meeting will be-held to- morrow afternoon in connection with , the Wycliffe P.S.A. Mr. F. Collinson, of Manches- ter, will speak, and a Band of Hope Choir will render selections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Death of Mr. Edward Dennett.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. Edward Dennett.</p>
        <p>• We regret to state that Mr. Edward Dennett, of Martinscroft, died at his home on Wednesday. He was highly-respected in the neighlyinrhood. and much sympathy will be felt for his wife and family. Mr. Dennett was a member of the Board of Guardians, the Rural District Council and the Rural District Tribunal. He was elected by the electors of Martinscroft in suc- cession to Mr. William, Houghton. The funeral takes place this afternoon at Lymm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Shaw They;lis's Charity.</head>
        <p>Shaw They;lis's Charity.</p>
        <p>Blankets and other comforts were Clistributed to the aged poor of the Borough of Warrington on Saturday, at the office of the Charity In- vestigation Society, Bank-street. The distribu- tion was made by Mrs. Joseph Harrison, one of the trustees of the Shaw Thewlis's Charity, assisted by several ladies, and in accordance with the provisions of the trust the gifts were made without regard to religious belief of the applicants. The amount available this year was £l2, us. Bd., and 80 4.l.Dplicants were supplied.</p>
        <p>Warrington Musical Society's Concert.</p>
        <p>A grand concert is announced by the War- rington Musical Society for November 25th, in the Parr Hall, the proceeds to go to the local War Funds. A special feature of the concert will be the first appearance in War- rington of Miss Astra Desmond, the principal contralto in the Carl Rosa Opera Company, who has attained an extraordinary reputation. At the solo piano will be Lady Crosfield, whose keen artistic taste and brilliancy of execution will make her contributions to the programme quite a musical treat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Successful Whist Drive.</head>
        <p>A Successful Whist Drive.</p>
        <p>The whist drive which recently took place in the Parr Hall in aid of the Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent Home realised .E6l 14s. id. There were about 500 present, and 99 tables were occupied. The prize winners were :—Ladies: Mrs. W. Howard, Mrs. F. Lowe, Miss B. Jump, Miss .L. Kilgallon, Mrs. J. R. Turner, Miss A. Williams. Gentlemen: Mr. E Kelly, Mr. J. Mann, Miss B. Brown, Mr. Chadwick, Mr. J. Cross, Mr. J. Raymond. Mrs. F. A. Frost thanks all those who assisted to make the drive so successful. , •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Dr. Bennett's First-Aid Lectures.</head>
        <p>Dr. Bennett's First-Aid Lectures.</p>
        <p>The first of a series of first-aid lectures under the auspices of the British Red Cross Society was given to ladies in Cairo-street School on Thursday evening. Dr. Bennett, of Padgate, was the lecturer, and there was a good audience present, most of them signing their names to take the whole course, with a view to more serious work in the future, and not purely to attend out of curiosity. The students were drawn from all parts of the town. The course is for five weeks, and will be continued next Thursday. Miss Joynson, 7, Museum-street, is the secretary for the course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Memorial Service at Bewsey Road Church.</head>
        <p>Memorial Service at Bewsey Road Church.</p>
        <p>At the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening an _impressive memorial service</p>
        <p>to the late frivates William Coyne, Albert Hughes and James Simpson, formerly members of the church and Sunday school, was con- ducted by the Rev. W. T. Derry. There was a, very large congregation. The reverend gentle- man took as his text "Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends," and spoke in glowing terms of the loyalty and self-sacrifice of the three young men. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- The- hymns sung included "Now the lab- ourer's task is o'er," "For all the saints," and "0 God, our help in ages past." Mr. G. Pendlebury presided at the organ and played "0 rest in the Lord," and the Dead March in "Saul."</p>
        <p>Raddon Court Pound Day. The usual monthly Pound Day was held at Raddon Court Convalescent Hospital on Thurs- day. A large number of visitors were present, and brought with them various gifts, including groceries, fruit, biscuits, vegetables, butter and cigarettes. An exhibition of handicraft by the patients—in which the successful competitors were elected by the vote's of their comrades— was lion by Lance-Corporal Thomas and Private Flitton. A concert was held during the after- noon, at which songs were given by Mrs. Park- house and Private Grime, who also gate a duet, the patients joining heartily in the chorus. Miss Reynolds gave selections on the violin, and Private Stone, a patient at the Warrington In- firmary. on the mandoline. Tea was served in the hall. It may be stated that the patients of all the military hospitals in Warrington are to enter an exhibition of handicraft to be held in the Parr Hall on Dedember 19th.</p>
        <p>Fellow-Workmen's, Tribute to the Late Mr. Silas Shutt.</p>
        <p>There was rather a remarkable tribute of respect shown by his fellow-workmen at the funeral on Tuesday of Mr. Silas Shutt, who for many years worked in the Scrap Forge at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. Mr. Shutt was taken ill while work- ing as a shingler on Saturday morning, and passed away in the lodge before medical aid could arrive. By his workmates and the management he was highly esteemed, and when the funeral cortege passed over Bewsey Bridge, from his lodgings in Lovely-lane, the work of his department was suspended, and \ the men and women employed therein lined up on both sides of the road with bared heads. Their floral tribute was also conspicuous among the many wreaths sent. The interment took place at Cemetery, the last rites being performed by the Rev. T. T. James. Mr.. P. Boardman, a manager at Bewsey Forge, was present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>'Soldier Addresses Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>'Soldier Addresses Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday, pre- sided over by Mr. 13. Farrar, who gave a hearty welcome to Interpretor Le-Holder, of White- cross Hospital, also to Brother Wm. Rutter, bandsman, South Lancashire Regiment, who was over on a short visit. The Chairman read a letter from Private W. J. Horrocks, South. Langs., who wrote from the trenches and ex- pressbd the hope that when the war was over he would have the old happy times at the P.S.A. over again. Mr. Farrar voiced a vote of sympathy to Mrs. Joynson and family in the death of leir. Joynson, an old member of the P.S.A.; also to Mr. W. H. Ilallington„ in the death of his brother, Thos. Wallington, of Northwich, who was for some years secretary of Wycliffe P.S.A.; also the the daughters of Mr. Thos. Wallington, who are left to mourn the loss of their father. Private Price, of South Lancs., gave the address upon "Influence," with strong reference to brotherly care, syzn- pathy and help to all around us. and especially to those whose relatives have. fallen. Mr. Jos. Poole gave two solos in a pleasing manner, "In His steps" and "My hope is in the Everlast- ing." Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. Mr. C. Cotton and Mr. Wm. Beswick also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Student.</head>
        <p>Student.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath, October, 1916. Dear Sir,—l. am pleased to be able to inform you that I have been successful • in securing a position in a local office as the result of your kind recommendation and interest in my commercial welfare. I shall always look back with pleasure upon the splendid training I have received in the Day Classes, and shall always recommend the Col- lege whenever I have the opportunity:.—Yours sincerely.</p>
        <p>Copied from the original by he proprietors of this paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Requiem Mass for Roman Catholic</head>
        <p>Requiem Mass for Roman Catholic</p>
        <p>Soldiers.</p>
        <p>On Sunday a solemn Requiem Mass was cele- brated in St. Mary's. Church, .Buttermarket- street, for the repose of the souls of men who had fallen during the war. Amongst those pres- ent was the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock). A satafalque had been erected in the chancel, and the service was most impressive. The Mass was sung by Father Wilson, while Father Hind was deacon and Father Hayes sub-deacon. .Father Hind preached the sermon, and impressed the object of the service upon the large congrega- tion present. Mr. J. Eckersley was the organist, and the Gregorian music of the Mass was ren- dered with befitting solemnity.</p>
        <p>Happenings at Bold Street Wesleyan Churcp. To-morrow is the sixty-sixth anniversary of the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, and the preacher, morning and evening, will be the Rev. A. Leathley Heap, of Leeds. In the afternoon his Worship the Mayor will preside, and the Rev. W. T. Derry will speak. Special music will be rendered, Miss Eva Rich, soprano (Sheffield Musical Festival) being one of the soloists.</p>
        <p>To-night (Saturday), at 6.45, the electric lighting installation having been completed, it will be formally opened by Mrs. rrederick. Pendlebury, the Rev. W. T. Derry presiding. On Thursday afternoon the Rev. Howard May, of Inverness, will preach, and in the evening lecture on "New Age Junction—All Change !" Mr. F. E. Penketh will preside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Insured Persons: Change of.Doetor</head>
        <p>Insured Persons: Change of.Doetor</p>
        <p>The attention of insured pers— ons is called to the notice in our advertisement columns respecting change .of doctor at the end of the year. Those who wish to make a change of doctor must give notice in writing to the Clerk to the Insurance Committee, and must also surrender their medical cards. In order to pro- tect, as far as possible, the lists of those doctor; who are on active service with his Majesty's Forces, anyone wishing to transfer from the list of such a doctor will require to furnish the Clerk with a full statement as to the reasons why they desire to change. The Medical Ser- vice Sub-committee will then decide as to whether the application should be granted or not.</p>
        <p>The notices must be sent to the Clerk before the Ist December. In the absence of such notices, insured persons will be deemed to have decided to remain on their present doctor's lists for another 12 months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Rev. W. A. Beckett at Wesley Guild</head>
        <p>Rev. W. A. Beckett at Wesley Guild</p>
        <p>Rally.</p>
        <p>The annual rally of the Wesley Guilds in the Warrington Circuit was held at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Thursday evening. There was a fair attendanc,e, Mr. C. Wilcockson pre- siding. The General Secretary's report was read by Mr. K. Bishop. The shield this year having been won by the Stockton Heath Guild, Mr. W. E. Plinston, Padgate, presented it to Mrs. Wilcockson, who received zt on behalf of the Guild. Mr. Wilcockson, in calling upon the Rev. W. A. Beckett, a former minister of the circuit, to deliver an address, said that the best part of being able to get former ministers to come was that they needed no introduction. The Rev. W. A. Beckett, in an able and in- teresting address, said that we often heard the song, "Keep the home fires burning." The fires that should burn at home, he said,- should be of love and hope. We should also keep the Church fires burning brightly. We were living in troublous times, and needed to live close to Gad. The collection was devoted to the Soldiers' Home, Bold-street. In the afternoon the Rev. W. A. Beckett delivered a sermon, the congre- gation adjourning to the schoolroom for tea.</p>
        <p>Nursing' Association. The annual, meeting of subscribers and friends of the Warrington District Nursing As- sociation will be held on Monday after- noon in. the • Town Hall at three o'clock, under the presidency of his Worship the Mayor. The nineteenth annual report shows a record of excellent work during the past year, and Coun. H. Roberts, hon. treasurer, in his report states that the financial requirements have been adequately provided for. The el•••'-' have slightly increased -</p>
        <p>Workin^: praise. the ui was</p>
        <p>.an the</p>
        <p>And includ-</p>
        <p>effo. Subscript</p>
        <p>esults of the ,ather,, Committee. by nearly £s—amount</p>
        <p>£259 15s. Co i. emphasises the fact that the association is maintained for he purpose of nursing the poor free af charge in their own homes, and that in all other cases where the services of their nurses are desired the very' reasonable scale of fees fixed by the committee for such services must be paid. The con; mittee's report chronicles 13,074 visits to 5S° cases, amongst these being 106 cases Of wives; children and dependents of men who serve(' or are serving in his Majesty's Forces. The report of the ladies' committee is couched in appreciative terms of services rendered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Death of Mrs. Elizabeth A. Green.</head>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Elizabeth A. Green.</p>
        <p>Deep feeling and widespread regret wag manifested on Saturday at the funeral of Mrs' Elizabeth Green, 60, Laira-street. Mrs. Green ,had been conveyed to St. Mary's Hospital, Manchester, to receive expert treatment for an internal complaint. An operation was found necessary and was performed on October 30th. It was, however, unavailing, and she Passed, a*ay upon the same day. Mrs. Green ha" enjoyed the esteem of many friends and neigh; hours, to whom she rendered much service and kindness, and very real sorrow was manifested when the news of her death reached Warring- ton. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday afternoon. There W." a large and sympathetic gathering of friends and neighbours and the service was impres: sively read by the Rev. B Percival, vicar of St. Peter's. Among the chief mourners were; Mr. Samuel Green (husband), Messrs. J. ana T. Greenhalgh (brothers), Mr. J. Green (father- in-law), Messrs. J. Potter and T. Waring (brothers-in-law), Mrs. J. Lee, J. Cain, and J. Kmern.dsa.llw(riuttsainkse)r, aMnrd. 3Df.r.NFoileeant,wMoord..M. Messrs. Y. Monnfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements. Firth,</p>
      </div>
