      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>KILLED.</head>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE E. GRICE.</p>
        <p>Officer Pays Him A Fine Tribute.</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Grice, South Lancashire Regi- ment, fell in action on October 2.2nd, and his mother, , who resides at 39, Lythgoes-lane, has received a letter front his officer. "As his Platoon Commander," he wrote, " I should like to take this opportunity of rendering a tribute to his never-failing, perseverance and good spirits —even in the worst of times.".</p>
        <p>Private Once, who was a former employe of the Vulcan Foundry, was in the Army when war broke out. His brother, John, is still serv- ing, while 'another,. Harry, has been discharged as unfit for further Fen- iee</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>PRIVATE T. FART:F.LI...</head>
        <p>PRIVATE T. FART:F.LI...</p>
        <p>Died Viith His Fan To Th::</p>
        <p>Foe.</p>
        <p>The t:vo following letters have been written to Mrs. C. Hines, -15, Cressley-Areet, tHing the death in action et' her son, Priv:, Thorna- Fart:ell,. South Lance Regiment. Lance-Sergeant joitc, writing on uctier 10th, says:—" I an +0 have to, ine- -Private</p>
        <p>-ori.y to have to inforr</p>
        <p>you ot your son deatn, Machine Gun Section I feel his gr was a good chum an( ,iic ld• and had a good word rev oody. killed on October Ir,t: ,e suffered no pain, . I was there when he did. He died a hero fol</p>
        <p>i'arrll</p>
        <p>los,. He</p>
        <p>die!.</p>
        <p>He got- nd</p>
        <p>PRIVATE Tl-10. FARRELL.</p>
        <p>Killid.</p>
        <p>• his King and country. and lies in peace at the \ back of our lines With of his comrades, wilt, get killed along with Ilia,. The ofticefs, N.C.O.'s and men of his conapit ?iv feel his los,s and s:!nd.</p>
        <p>•, their deepest syropathy•— The ,ieennd letter, Iron, Corporal Lomax, on October 13th, ran Oin part):—" I ant sure yon will be proud to know that he (beet in action</p>
        <p>BiA alnd he Ava:: ;Away,</p>
        <p>with his face towan.k .the foe myself miss him very 'much good company, rade in distress." Enlisting on the outbrenk of wi-4r. Priviate Farrell wa:4 drafted out to. the Dardanelles, and</p>
        <p>ud r r</p>
        <p>help It corn-</p>
        <p>was invalided honie in November. of last year with dysentery. After a spell in hospital he was' sent to France -even months ago. He for-</p>
        <p>nierly worked tar the Whitecross Co., Ltd., was educated at St. .Marv's F.C. Schcol, t‘nd was. 26 years of age. His brother, 'Gunner Peter Farrell, R.F.A.,. is 'serving in Mesopotamia. while his half-hrother, Privat,. InnPs, is with the South Lancashires in training.</p>
