      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PRIVATE E. JACKSON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE E. JACKSON.</p>
        <p>Missing For Eighteen Months ; Now Rep9rted Dead.</p>
        <p>ard Mr‘,. . .Thek.en. street, have received official news from the War</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>Office to the effet that their son; Privat Edward Jackson, South Lancashbie Reginolo, has been .killed .in action. A comrade, I);.ivot., R. Sandhurst, says that he saw Private E. Jackson killed, along with two more,i of his comrades. He had beia missing sine 25th September of last year. Private Jackson enlisted on the 21st • July, 1914, and had been on active service eighteen :months, and was 19 year: of age. He was for- merly in the employ of Mr. Addis. bootmaker, :Sankey Green, anil attended Padgato Church..</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. HILL.</p>
        <p>Reported Killed After Fourteen</p>
        <p>Months' Silence.</p>
        <p>Reported as missing since August -9th Of last year, having fought throughout the Dardanelles campaign, Private James Hill. South Lanca- 'shire Regiment, and son of Mr. J. Hill, 44, Hopwood-street, is referred to in a comlnunica- lion by the Army Council stating their regret that they have to conclude that he met his -death on that date. Twenty-two years' of age and an "old boy" Of St. Ann's School, Private Hill was, up to the time of his enlistment, in the employ of the Yirth Company. His brother, Private William has been wounded on two* ooAsions.</p>
