      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>SUBSTITUTION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Principle Employed By The</head>
        <p>Principle Employed By The</p>
        <p>Tribunal.</p>
        <p>.Thursday's .Tribunal ,in the Town 'Hall was composed of Dr. Joseph (deputy mayor), Alder- man Tinnion, Mr. F. A. Fro,st, Mr. J. Elwell, and 3)-r. W. Bradbury. Captain Heaton was the military representative. In the case of a couple of firms who appealed for exemption for some of their employes the Tribunal refused the applications conditionally in order that the principle of substitution could be brought into operation. In several cases they reserved their decisions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Wire Weavers Exempted.</head>
        <p>Wire Weavers Exempted.</p>
        <p>An application • was made for exemption by Messrs. Thomas Locker and Co., Ltd., in favour of two wire weavers, one aged 26, married, no children, passed in C class, and the other aged 40, married, six children, who was passed for service in Class C (i.).—Conditional exemp- tion was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Case of Two Brothers.</head>
        <p>Case of Two Brothers.</p>
        <p>Two brothers, aged 19 and 20 'respectively, asked for exemption on the grounds of alleged dcrotz.,tic hardship. Tisey stated that they were the sole support of their-mother,. who is over 50 years. TheY were passed for _active service.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tim:lion .-(who presided) said the Tribunal considered that if they went it woul,l not come under the heading of extreme hard- ship. There was the Prince of Wales' Fund and a- very large sum. was collected in War- rington for the purpose. ' There might he a certain amount of hardship, but if one of the applicants made an allowance their mother would get 12s. 6d. weekly, and the Clerk of the Tribunal would do what he could to get 'some- thing for her from the Prince of Wales' Fund. The elder brother would have, to go at once and the ounger brother. woulii be given, exemption until the end of theyear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LOC 1L lIELPERS.</head>
        <p>LOC 1L lIELPERS.</p>
        <p>If our readers need encouragement and help, surely both will be found ,in such an earnest message as the one we are privileged to give to-day. It is from a local man, and rings with sincerity. Read it and bear in 'mind that it is only one of many such stirring messages. •On March 16th, 1915, Mr. T. Smith, of 33, Melville-street, Warrington, said :—"For fully three months I was so crippled with lumbago that I had to walk about with my back bent like an old man. The 'pain used to catch me right at the bottom of my back and strike down my loins. It was quite like a knife going through me, and I became so bad that I hardly dared move. How I-managed to keep about, I don't know. I was in pain all the time, day and night, and continual suffering almost wore me out. I tried plasters and embrocations, and I got a belt which cost me five guineas, but I am sorry to say I got no relief. I began to think I should never get well again. "It was through reading a testimonial that I tried Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. I had not taken many dos of these pills before I found that they were relieving me. Needless to say, I persevered with them, and it was not long before I was quite cured. Doan's Pills have been worth their weight in gold to me, and 1 am sure they would be to anyone who suffers with a bad back." (Signed) "T. Smith." On February 17th, 191&amp;—eleven months later— Mr. Smith said:—" I have never had any serious return of lumbago since Doan's Pills made such a fine cure of me. I feel I can't say too much in their praise." Lumbago is rheumatism of the muscles of the back and loins. You wake up in the mornings with a crippling pain in the babk, and sharp pains dart through the loins whepts stooping or rising from a chair. Lumbago is aUsed by uric acid irritating and inflaming the muscles or nerVes of the loins. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills help the kidneys to throw off the poisonous acid through the urinary system. Of all dealers, or 2/9 a box, from Foster- McClellan Co., 8. Wells---street, Oxford-street. London, W. Don't ask for backache or kidney pills,—ask distinctly for Doe'res Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Smith had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>CRUSHED TO DEATH.</head>
        <p>CRUSHED TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>Fatal Accident To A Carter At Walker's Brewery.</p>
        <p>, PJ'</p>
        <p>: 4</p>
        <p>. 4'</p>
        <p>. , , v</p>
        <p>, • f, -t 4* t. t</p>
        <p>On. Monday morning a teamslnan.named Wil- liam Henry Jackson was crushed to death between the shaft of v. lurry- and a wall at Messrs. Peter Walker and Son's brewery, Dal- lam-lane. The Circuingtaiicewere.,investigated the following day by Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, and a verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>Joseph John Jackson, of Bolton, identified the body as that of his brother, aged 27 years, who resided with his wife and two children at Park View, Bold. Mrs:Webster, who carries on businvs,slit Park View, Bold, stated that Jackson was employed by her as a townsman. Part of his work was to go a couple of times each week to -Walker's Brewery for grains, and he had been going regu- larly- for the past twenty months. The horse was a quiet one.</p>
        <p>William Taylor, farmer, Brow Farm, Sankey, deposed that about 9.45 on Monday he saw a horse and lurry underneath the shoot being loaded with grains. When the lurry was loade4, Jackson took bold of the bridle with his right hand, and the horse in trying to start slipped on. the setts. Jackson dianged hands and caught hold of the bridle' with his left hand. While walking backwards the near side shaft pinned him against the wall. Witness ran to the horse .9.na backed it away, and deceased fell .to the ground. . Witness spoke to Jackson, but he did not answer. He was subsequently taken away in the ambulance.</p>
        <p>Detective Officer Turner stated that he wit to the Irfirmary and saw deceased. There. was an extensive injury between the man's right aru. and the right side of his heart. The doctor said -that he appeared to have some ribs broken, and that the right lung was ruptured. The jury expressed the view that death was accidental.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles W. Lee, solicitor (Messrs. H. Greenall and Co.), who represented Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, said the firm were,extremely sorry to hear of this sad accident to the de- ceased and desired to convey to the widow and her family their sincere sympathy, The Foreman asked that the jury should be associated with this expression of sympathy.</p>
        <p>jt-411;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
