      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LYMM</head>
        <p>LYMM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>If concert promoters would only have a look round our munition shops they would be sure to find something to their advantage.</p>
        <p>I overheard two Statham munitioneers sing- ing a duet the other day, and believe me, I was fairly surprised.</p>
        <p>I never dreamt that such talent could remain undiscovered.</p>
        <p>Another case was that of a lady sinjing the popular Scotch melody, "Annie Laurie." I could not have expected anything better at one of the Halle .concerts.</p>
        <p>Several deaths have taken place during the week, and Lymm is, one Thy one, losing all its old inhabitants.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most noticed of them all is the death of Mr. Edward Taylor, which took place very suddenly early last Saturday morning.</p>
        <p>A few days before his death the deceased gentleman was apparently in the best of health, for as late as the Thursday before he was out at a party.</p>
        <p>He was taken seriously ill and succumbed, following an operation.</p>
        <p>He was an ex-member`of the V.T.C., a mem- ber of the Conservative Club, and a prominent golfer.</p>
        <p>In business he was the manager of the Man- chester district for Tate's, the well-known sugar importers.</p>
        <p>He was widely respected in the district, and his loss *ill be greatly felt by ,a large circle of friends.</p>
        <p>He was only in his 42nd year, and ray greatest sympathy, goes to his widow.</p>
        <p>As a token of deepest respect a deputation of the local plato9n of •Volunteers attended ,his funeral in uniform, with' the second- button draped.</p>
        <p>Ad the flag over the golf pavilion hung at half-mast.</p>
        <p>• By the way, what wretched weather we are haying at present. I wonder when it is going to cease raining.</p>
        <p>If this weather continues our farmers will he advancing their prices again and telling us -the weather is responsible. •</p>
        <p>It. is getting quite a long time now since we heard of Manchester being short of water.</p>
        <p>If they only had two or three days' supply at the time of their scarcity, then they ought to have three or four years' supply by now.</p>
        <p>The Tradesmen's Association held their annual meeting last Tuesday night in the Hirst'S Pavilion.</p>
        <p>• All last year s officials were re-elected which clearly shows that the members place in them their greatest confidence.</p>
        <p>Although Mr. David Daniels, the president, objected to re-election, the members were not of the same opinion, and they re-elected him unanimously.</p>
        <p>They. 'have decided to make a special effort on behalf of,the British Red Cross Society, and it is to take the form of a concert. -</p>
        <p>I hear that their, hou secrefary, who out- lined this scheme, has his eye on some very good talent.</p>
        <p>Perhaps he is going to give some of our local artiste § a chance.</p>
        <p>The little village is now getting quite up-to- date. I noticed our first uniformed postwoman last week-end.</p>
        <p>She looks so smart that she makes,some of the men feel quite jealous.</p>
        <p>Ffow have von got on with the Early Closing Order? I hope you did not go hungry last Monday evening.</p>
        <p>I heard of one man who did, although he was hardly to blame.</p>
        <p>He was a boatman, and only landed in Lymm just as the clock struck eight.</p>
        <p>He rushed to the nearest shop only in time to see the door being closed, and •hear the shop- keeper shout " Times up."</p>
        <p>That is what I call a fine point of law. Lymm was the first place where he could obtain a loaf of bread and because he only just landed as the clock struck eight he had to go hungry until next morning.</p>
        <p>The Millstone I nn.—The.licence of the Mill.- stone Inn has been transferred from Edward Forsyth to James Alfred Atkinson.</p>
        <p>Our Day: Result of Concert—The total pro- ceeds of the concert held in the Drill Hall a fortnight ago by the Working Men's .Com- mittee and rendered by the 'Queries in Querie7_ land," amounted to £l6 6s. 11d. No expenses whatever will be deducted from this sum, 'but will be met by private individuals.</p>
        <p>Waterman and His Registration , Card.—At Runcorn, on Monday, Thomas Shaw (18), waterman, of Newfield Cottages, Lymm, and also of the canal boat Julia, was charged with having failed to produce his registration card when requested to do so by a police-constable. He pleaded guilty.—P.C. Powis stated that he asked defendant for his registration card, and he replied that he had not been registered. Defendant now produced his registration card. He said that he had been registered, but had not the card with him when the officer inter- viewed nominal fine of Is. was in- flicted.</p>
        <p>Private Fred Percival Loses All His Teeth.— Private Fred Percival, the Cheshire Regiment, who is with the Salonica Expeditionary Force, writing to his wife at Moston-street, Newfields, from a dental ward convalescent hospital, says he has lost all his teeth. He has been on active service for over 18 intmths, commencing his career in France. He was wounded in the Battle of Loos, and soon after recovering was drafted out to Salonica. A few months ago he contracted fever. Up to the present he has been in hospital a litle over three months. He is a married man with two children. Prior to enlisting he was employed by the Grap- penhall Tanning Co. His brother-in-law, Private Arthur Statham, also of the Cheshire Regiment, who was ;wounded about four months , ago, and is at present, recuperating, -in the: Harrogate Military Hospital, is well on the road to recovery, and hopes to be home at an early date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MR. ED NA ARD TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>MR. ED NA ARD TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Sudden Death Of A Popular Resident.</p>
        <p>It is with the deepest regret that we announce the death of Mr. Edward Taylor, which took place at his residence, Statham-avenue, early last Saturday morning, after a very brief ill- ness. The deceased gentleman as Most popu- lar" in the village, and had a very large circle of friends, by whom he will be deeply missed. Though only in his 42nd year, he was one of our leading Manchester business men, and occupied the important position of general manager in Manchester and district ;for Henry Tate and Sons, sugar importers and refiners. Up to a few days previous to his death he was apparently in the best of health, but on Friday evening he became seriously ill and, despite the best of medical skill and attention, he sue- Cumed early on Saturday morning, following an operation. He was an ex-member of the V.T.C., a popular member of the Conservative Club and Association, and a prominent mem- ber of the Lymm Golf Club. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Funeral.</head>
        <p>The Funeral.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Parish Church on Tuesday afternoon, and was witnessed by a large number of representatives and friends. The service was fully choral, and the church choir wis. in attendance. The body was con- veyed from Statham-avenue to its last resting place in a hearse, which was followed by two mourning coaches conveying the only relative present and his most immediate. friends. At the cortege was passing the Dam Head a deputation of the local platoon of Volunteers, in charge of Lieut. G. R. Williamson and Sergeant S. ,D. Mos- ton (he was an ex-member), lined the road. As the procession passed they were called to "Attention." The principal mourners were:— Brother-in-law (who represented the family), and Messrs. S. H. Davies, J. Slee, Bradbury, T. Wilson, Dr. (or Capt.) W. littley, J. IL Atherton, Abraham Omerod, F. Garside, A. H. Evans, and a large number Of friends and repre- sentatives. Messrs. Henry Tate and Sons were represented by their head cashier from the Liverpool district. After the graveside service was completed the choir sang "Lord, now lettest Thou Thy servant depart in peace, and the large crowd dwindled away, taking with them sweet memories of a gentleman who was every- one's friend. The Rector performed the funeral, ceremony, and the 'church organist, Mr. W. Cooke, played suitable " funeral music. The large number of floral tributes presented a pretty spectacle, and so many were they that they had to be conveyed to the churchyard in special cars. The donors were :—" His widow," "Sister Annie and Brother Harry," "Brothers Bob and Charlie, Father and Mona," "Will,:Min. and Mona," "Joe," "Lydia and Charlie," Harry and Julia Wright, the staff from Henry Tate and Sons, Ltd., members of the Conservative Club, "A few friends from the 'Ditch," Lymm Golf -Club, Mr. and Mrs. 'Slee, Mr. and Mrs. S. H. Davies, Mr. and Mrs. Bradbury, Mr. and Mrs. T. Wilson, Dr: and Mrs. Uttley, J. H. Atherton, Mr. and Mrs Abraham Omerod, G. Shelmerdine and staff, Mr. and Mrs. F. Garside, Messrs. E., Rogers and E. Dalton, Mr. and Mrs. Tweedale, Mr. and Mrs. W. 1% Moulton, Mr. and Mrs. M. B. Scott, "Daisy Barker and Regi- nald," Edith S. Barker, "His two little friends, Kathleen and Leonard," Mr. and Mrs. W. Cooke.</p>
        <p>The furieral arrangements were conducted by Mr. S. H. Davies, for- Mrs. Taylor, and Messrs. Kendal, Milne, of Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ANOTHER " FIELD " DAY.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER " FIELD " DAY.</p>
        <p>D.C.M. Hero Receives Another Decoration.</p>
        <p>Ex-Quaytermaster-Sergeant T. W. Field, our Gallipoli D.C.M. hero, has been awarded a fur- ther honour for his conspicuous gallantry in the form of the Russian Medal, -the Order of St. George (Ist Class). This refreshes our memories and makes us think again of our other departed hero, Lance-Sergeant Henry Brookes, who, it will be remembered, was awarded the same honour for conspicuous gallantry in performing ambulance work. The only difference between the two medals is that the one awarded Brookes was of the fourth class, and is of silver, while the one awarded Field is of the first class, and is of gold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>PRIVATE WALTER MIRE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE WALTER MIRE.</p>
        <p>Ten British Shells For Every German</p>
        <p>Shell.</p>
        <p>Writing to his mother at Booth's-lane, Cherry- lane, Private Walter Make .says:—" Just a line to let- you know that I am still in the pink. Although we have had such very bad weather during the last three or four months it is gratifying to know that most of us have come through all right. I have lost a few good pals, but they have done their bit, and I can assure you we have had a time that we shall never forget. We have done very well, and have accounted for a few Germans. You can see them lying about everywhere, so you can guess what it is like out here. There was not much fight left in them when our artillery had finished with them, for they have done splen- didly.l am sure that for every single shell they send over to us we returned them ten post free. Most of them were dazed 'by the bom- bardment; they are just getting a taste of what our lads got at the commencement of war. We have been in this place now for about a month, and it is getting pretty quiet. They are not giving us much trouble. I am expecting\ to be home on leave very soon. I did think I should have got -over for this week-end, but they are not granting leave to as many at once now. so I expect I shall have to wait about a month. But, never mind, it will come some day (with a bit Of luck). I suppose you often wonder how I am getting On out here; but you must mot worry, for I am 'AI, and quite happy. We take things as they oome out here, which I think is the hist thing to do in a game like this. The war is bound to finish some day, and, as we often say to one another, 'The first seven years are always the worst.' We' are having some wet weather again now, and it makes the trenches very bad.• A chum of mine 'went up to his neck last night in what we call a sump hole, and needless to say he got' ' some ' wet and used ' some ' language." _ . .</p>
        <p>Private W. Maire- answered his group call early in the year. He has been on active ser- vice in France now for several months, and is a member of the Cheshire Regiment. In -civil life he was a farm labourer, employed by a Thelwall farmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Lymm Platoon: No. 1 Battalion. Orders for week ending November 18th :</p>
        <p>Tuesday, Nov. 14th.-7.30 p.m.: Recruits' Squad Drill; 8 p.m.: Guard Mounting and Re- lieving Sentries.</p>
        <p>Thursday. NOY. 16th.--7.30 -t).m.: Recruits' Squad'Drill with arras;.B pan.: Platoon Drill. Orderjies for the week.—Sergt. Wood, Ptes Power and Mercer.</p>
        <p>By Order, G. R. WILLIAMSON, Lieut.</p>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PRIVATE AN IMAM TAYLOR'S COMPLIMENT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE AN IMAM TAYLOR'S COMPLIMENT.</p>
        <p>Only Paper He Is Interested In Reading</p>
        <p>—" The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Writing to his sister at Moston-street, New- fields, Private Wm. Taylor, one of our local soldiers with the Mesopotamian Expeditionary Force, says :—"lf we stop out here much longer we shall be as black as niggers, for we are getting some very hot weather; in fact, much too hot for white men, but thank God I am in the best of health. Tell mother that lam getting all her letters and papers. I get one or two by every .mail that comes. It is very nice out here, I can assure you, to open 'The Examiner' and see a bit of Lymm news.- It brings back many recollections of the dear old village; in fact it is the only. paper that I am interested in reading out here. I enclose a letter from Jim."</p>
        <p>. Private Wm. Taylor's numerous interesting letters have been published in this paper from time to time. The brother Jim refererd to is the Mr. James Taylor, who emigrated to Canada several years ago. This is what he tells his brother : •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>22 Degrees Below Zero.</head>
        <p>22 Degrees Below Zero.</p>
        <p>"I see you are giving the Germans now. Just keep it up and then the war will soon be over. I have not been in the best of health- lately, but, thank God, I am getting all right again. I would like to be over there with you just for a change. •Keep on smiling and giving it them hot and we will all have a good time together some day. This is a very cold place .in winter, in fact it was just 22 degrees below zero when I landed. We have been having a touch of war just recently. They killed about twenty men last month., That's the way they do it here, and then they think that they have done something wonderful. Good-bye and good luck, hoping you come through all right. —Your well wishing brother, Jim."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TRADESMEN'S ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>TRADESMEN'S ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Satisfactory Reports For The Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Lyman Tradesmen's Association took place in the Hirst's Pavilion on Tuesday night, and was presided over by Mr. David Daniels, president. There were also present Messrs. W. E. Hillier (hon. sec.), A. B. Richardson (lion. treasurer), 11. S. Saville, J. Rogerson, G. Bowen, J. Shepherd, Levi Whoinason, Wm. Allman, H. Hirst and J. H. Warham.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to Mr. J:H. Warham for his untiring efforts in supplying an augmented band for the procession on Citizen's Sunday, and the Secretary, was in- structed to send a letter of thanks to Mr. Alex. Hulme, the bandmaster. The year ended with a balance in hand of £272, with a membership of 50, fully paid. Four resignations were accepted during the year.</p>
        <p>It was decided that in future the lowest tender for printing would be accepted, even if it came from an outside firm.</p>
        <p>Two auditors are to be elected at the August meeting. It was also decided that on the Wednesday following Christmas Day all shops will remain open until 7 p.m.</p>
        <p>-A scheme for a concert in aid of the Red Cross Society, as outlined by the Hon. Secretary, will be carried through. It was decided to continue to send parcels to the Association's " adopted" prisoner of war, Private Walter Massey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Election of Officers.</head>
        <p>Election of Officers.</p>
        <p>Mr. David Daniels, the retiring president, in giving up the chair, thanked the members for their most loyal support, but he was im- mediately proposed for re-election. Mr. Daniels objected, as he had held this office for four years-. The proposition was, however, im- mediately seconded and carried unanimously. Mr. A. B. Richardson was also re-elected hon. treasurer, and he thanked-the meeting for again placing their confidence in 'him. Mr. W. E. Hillier was re-elected hon. secretary, and in thanking the members for their loyal support, he stated that he would always give the Association his best..</p>
        <p>• The Vice-nresidents were re-elected. On the Executive Council, Mr. Bowen was elected to take the place of Mr. Odber, who has left the district. and Mr. Levi Thomason was</p>
        <p>elected in the place of Mr. J. W.' Gibbon, who has retired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Church of England Waifs and Strays.— Special sermons in aid of the Diocesan Branch of the Church of England Waifs' ,and Strays' Society were preached in Christ Church, Pad- gate, on Sunday, by the Rev. H. Johnson, who showed how,. in spite of there being" so many demands upon the public at present, the in- come for the Society had experienced a sub- stantial increase. At present they were look- ing after 4,000 children, and since its founda- tion over 20,000 children had been rescued. The collections were in advance of last year.</p>
        <p>The Churches and the War Regulations.—lt was announced in Christ Church on Sunday that in order to comply with the regulations of the police, the church bell would not be rung in future for Sunday evening service, although the service would be held as usual at 6.30. For several weeks the services in the Wesleyan Church have been held in the afternoon, but arrangements have now been made to comply with lighting regulations, and in future the evening service will be held at six o'clock as before.</p>
        <p>Tennis and Bowling Club.—The annual whist- "drive and presentation of priz.was held in the pavilion on Thursday evening, presided over by Mr. E. Lunt (president of the -cub). 'Mrs. Lunt very kindly 'presented the prizes to the ,follow-. ing winners</p>
        <p>Tennis Cup: Ladies, Miss E. London; gentle- men, Mr. J. P. Duckworth.</p>
        <p>• Bowling: Dr. Bennett's cup, Mr. R. Tunstall; President's prize, Mr. J. Seed; Chairman's prize (Mr. Mitchell), Mr. J. Smith; Vice- president's prizes (Mr. T. B. Duckworth) 1; Mr. J. Appleton; 2, Mr. R. Tunstall; Vice-president's prizes (Mi.. P. IL Bennett), 1; Mr. E. Lunt; 2, Mr. W. Mitchell; Captain's prizes (Mr. J. Appleton), 1, Mr. B. Warburton; 2, Mr. J. Angus; Single Bowling Handicaps, 1, Messrs. R. Tunstall and R. Hankinson; 2, Messrs. J. Appleton and W. Wood; Double Bowling Handi- cap, Mr. T. Marsden and Mr. R. W. Cook; Bowling Drive, Mr.-.T. T. Hopkins. Whist Prizes (Ladies) : 1, Mrs. Lunt; 2, Mrs. Hankey. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. J. Smith; 2, Mr. F. Seed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Eastwood acted as M.C. for whist drive, and at the close thanks were accorded to the President, Mrs. Lunt, the Secretary (Mr. R. W. Cook) and Treasurer (Mr. Eastwood) for their services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>ORDERS.</head>
        <p>ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Parades.</p>
        <p>Sunday (to-morrow).—The Corps will parade and accompany his Worship the Mayor to the Parish Church. Fall in at 9.15 a.m. prompt at Headquarters, Enville-street.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Musketry Instruction, Drill, &amp;e., at 7.30.</p>
        <p>Friday.—Class Firing, 8 to 9 p.m.; Band Practice at 7 p.m. prompt. Oi•derlies Cadets Mills and Pattern. J. B. STARKEY, ; Commanding Officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Intention Good.</head>
        <p>Intention Good.</p>
        <p>Jim Smith was notoriously slow pay. He owed quite a bill at the grocery for pork. One day, as his 'credit was becoming strained, he walked calmly into the grocery and said: "Mr. Black, I want to pay you for the pork I have had, and I want some more."</p>
        <p>"Certainly," said the delighted proprietor, as he hastened to wait on his customer.</p>
        <p>Taking the package of pork, Jim Smith started to go. "Wait a minute," said the proprietor. ".1 thought you wanted to pay for the pork." "I do," remarked Jim, as he resumed hif homeward way, "but I can't."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>AN ARTFUL WOMAN.</p>
        <p>Enters Houses While Occupants</p>
        <p>Backs Are Turned.</p>
        <p>Having carried on a series of artful thefts for several months with impunity, Alice Dobson, a married woman, residing at 24, Derby-street, stood in the dock at the Borough Police Court, on Thursday, pleaded guilty, and was sentenced to six months' imprisonment. The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) explained that the prisoner's method was to discover that the occupants were engaged in the front rooms and then enter the premises at the back and take what money,and boots lay about in the kitchen. On one occasion the people were playing the piano in the front room. and Mrs. Dobson went into the kitchen and took what she could lay her hands on. On other occasions she saw the housekeepers leave to go into neighbouring shops, and, taking ad- vantage of their absence; she went into the houses at the rear. In all, she had got, in this way 195., besides many pairs of boots, and a silver watch. Nost of the boots had been recovered through pawnbrokers, but the money had gone. The burglaries traced totalled no fewer than seventeen, including two at houses in Cawdor-street, Stockton Heath. The streets in Warrington where she had been active were Lovely-lane, Norris-street, Orford-avenue, Thel- wall-lane, as well as several others. In con- clusion,' the Chief . Constable said that the *man alleged as her excuse that her husband gave her 30s. a week to keep the 'house going, and, as this was insufficient, she' had to go out pilfering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>SCHOOLBOY'S THEFT.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLBOY'S THEFT.</p>
        <p>Industrial School In Place Of Bad</p>
        <p>Home Surroundings.</p>
        <p>A small boy, ten years of age, was charged at the Juvenile Court on Wednesday with steal- ing 3s. 6d., the property of a little girl, from the Silver-street School.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Upson, headmaster of the school and also a special police sergeant, said that on Mon- day he was informed that the money had been taken off a desk, and as the boy had been seen. in the room he was questioned, but denied having touched the money. He was made to turn his pockets out and also take his clogs off, but the money was not found. Later, the boy came to witness with half a crown, saying he had found it, but this story was not credited, as :about two hundred and forty- children had passed over the :spot where, the boy. said he found the money. Ultimately he confessed that he had taken a shilling and slipped out of school and bought toffee and biscuits. He had also given 31d. for the wounded soldiers. _ Asked where the money was when he was searched, the boy said it was in his stockings. The boy had caused much trouble, and had been guilty of many small thefts at the school in the past. Mr. J. Moore Murray said the case was a very sad one, and he agreed with the proposal of the Chief Constable that it would be to the interests of the boy if he was sent away to a school. His home surroundings were anything but satisfactory, and the boy had slept out on three occasions.</p>
        <p>The boy was committed to an industrial school until he attained the age of sixteen years, his father to pay five shillings a week towards his maintenance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>DRINK AND WOUNDED SOLDIERS. Allegation Against Irish Farm Labourer.</head>
        <p>DRINK AND WOUNDED SOLDIERS. Allegation Against Irish Farm Labourer.</p>
        <p>On a charge of obtaining beer for a wounded soldier, Dominic Cryan, an Irish farm labourer, employed on a farm at Preston Brook, was re- manded at the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, before Mr. Thomas Baxter.</p>
        <p>P.C. C,ollier said that on the.previous Satur- day night he saw a wounded soldier loitering in the vicinity of a public-house at Dutton. 'con- cealing himself, witness saw the prisoner come out of the public-house and walk towards the wounded soldier. Later they entered a grocer's shop, and afterwards left together. They went towards a place where, there were some farm implements, and the prisoner produced a quart bottle and half-gallon bottle, containing beer. The quart bottle he gave to the soldier, and ,they proceeded in the direction of the work- house. The officer followed, and found the bottle containing /beer in the soldier's posses- sion. Asked why he }tad given the soldier the beer, prisoner replied that he had asked him to get it for him. He also pleaded with the wit- ness to let him off.</p>
        <p>PATRIOTISM AT A 4 DISCOUNT.</p>
        <p>On two charges of failing to produce a National Registration Card and of failing to report himself under the Military Service Act, Walter John Wood, of no settled abode, was fined 405., or in default a month's imprison- ment, and handed over to the Military</p>
