      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LYMM-PURE-SALT.</head>
        <p>LYMM-PURE-SALT.</p>
        <p>Extension Of A Patent For</p>
        <p>Purifying Brine.</p>
        <p>DR. TRANTOM'S ACHIEVE- MENT.</p>
        <p>The petition by Dr. William Trantom, of Grappenhall, Cheshire, and Messrs. Charles Moore and Company, Ltd., of Lymm, near War- rington, for the prolongation of their patent was heard in the Chancery Division before Mr. Jus- tice Sargant on the Ist, 2nd and 3rd of Novem- ber.</p>
        <p>The patent related to a process for the com- plete purification of brine, which enables the company to manufacture chemically pure sodium chloride on a large scale, which is sold under the name of Lymm-Pure-Salt," and guaranteed to contain 99.98% of sodium chloride. The grounds for the petition Were the merit torious nature of the invention, the commercial utility of the product, and the inadequate re- muneration of the petitioners. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Grant, Kki., and Mr. J. Hunter Gray appeared for the petitioners, and the Solicitor- General (Sir George Cave, K.C., M.P.) and Mr: J. Austin Cartmell for the Board of Trade.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grant explained that before this process was discovered, chemically pure sodium chloride could only be produced in very small quantities, and at a cost of about is. per lb. but Messrs. Charles Moore and Co., Ltd., had 13een able to put.their.product on the market akcom- merciai priees, which have enabled it to be used on a large scale for many purposes with great advantage to the community. The Solicitor-General, in stating the objec- tions to the petition on behalf of the Board of Trade, said there was no special or extraordin- ary merit in the inven".ion, Dr. Trantom hav- ing merely utilised common knowledge, and that the remuneration had been sufficient.</p>
        <p>•Dr. Trantom gave evidence rebutting the claims raised by the Board of Trade that the invention had been anticipated, and he enumer- ated the advantages obtained by the use of chemically pure salt in the preservation, of food products such as meat, bacon, butter, &amp;c., and in the curing of hides, and for other purposes. Evidence was also given as to the- reasons for delay in getting the full advantage of the patent, and it was shown that this delay was in no way due to the, patented process, but to other diffi- culties which had been since completely over- come.</p>
        <p>Mr.. A. Chaston Chapman, F.1.C., testified to the analysis of the product, and confirmed the guarantee of 99.98% given by the manufacturers. Mr. J. F. Pearson (Messrs. Pearson and Rut- ter, Ltd., butter importers), gave evidence in support of the claims as to the utility of " Lymm-Pure-Salt."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>" Of Wide Importance."</head>
        <p>" Of Wide Importance."</p>
        <p>His Lordship, in giving judgment, said that a great part of the process involved in the working of the patent put into practical operation know- ledge which formed part of the common stock of knowledge of chemists, though in one respect he was not sure that the inventor did not go beyond that and add to that common stock. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>The chief impurities in common salt were the sulphates and chlorides of lime and magnesia, and no chemist down to Dr. Trantom had been able to get rid of them so entirely, and he could not help feeling that the very simplicity of this invention .was one of the best proofs of its value. It was quite clear that there was extreme anxiety to get 'a really pure salt, but that in- ventors were all groping in the dark and occa- sionally getting hold of some part of the fringe of the question, but until Dr. Trantom appeared on the scene no one had got really to grips with it. One of his features had not been antici- pated, viz., that the material employed for the removal of the impurities should be used in a freshly precipitated condition. _ _</p>
        <p>He had evidence that the result was of wide importance, and thought the inventor and com- pany had made out their case, and that the _in- ventor, and through him the company, who are to-petitioners with him, have developed a pro- cess which is of very great importance to the public generally, and that they. had not been adequately remunerated.</p>
        <p>In all the circumstances he decided that the patent should be extended for three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Saturday, ,Nov. 18th: Monday,—Musketry at range, 7.30 p.m. Range Officer, Private F. W. Grounds. Tuesday.—Musketry Instruction. Fall in at Headquarters, 7.45 p.m.</p>
        <p>Thuraday.—Squad drill at headquarters, 7.45</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry at range for recruits, 7.30 p.m. Names of those required to attend will be, posted at Headquarters. Range Officer, Sec.- Commander W. Trantom.</p>
        <p>Saturday.—Company drill. Fall in at head- quarters at 3 p.m. Orderlies.—See-Corporal F. Fox, Private J. Wilkinson. .By order,</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST (Lieutenant) O.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Interesting Lecture in Wesleyan Schoolroom. —On Monday evening an interesting lecture was delivered in the. Wesleyan Schoolroom, Stockton Heath, in. connection :with the •Hill .Cliffe and StoOkton, Heath- :United Band of Hope. Mr. H. Bre-reton 'presided; and the lec- turer • was Mr. W. Watkins, who delighted his hearers with a description of the River Dee, following those pleasant waters from their source to the sea. The lecture was illustrated with over 80 slides, which included many charming views in North Wales and Chester. Portraits of the officials of the Warrington Band of Hope Union were thrown on the screen.. The lecture was, highly appreciated. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment In B.S.A.</p>
        <p>Competition.</p>
        <p>A team of the Stockton Heath Detachment of the 6th (Knutsford) Battalion of the. Cheshire Volunteer Regiment were drawn in the second round against "D " Company, Ist Battalion Norfolk Volunteers. The conditions were six men a-side, ten shots at a two-bull target, five on each, 11 inch black with a inch centre (10), possible score 100, at 25 yards, with open sights and no slings. As will be seen below, the local team lost the second round by the small margin of three points Scores • Stockton Heath.—A. Shepherd 96, R. M. Swin- ton 95, J. H. Fletcher 95, J. F. Fergusson 95, M. A. Piercy 87, J. 3lcDermid 81; total 519. 'Norfolk Volunteers.—Total ,552. On the same conditions a friendly match was shot against the team the Stockton Heath De- tachment were drawn against in the first round, and this time came out winners by four points only. Scores : • Stockton Heath.—J. F; Fergusson 95, J. Me- Dermid 95, J. H Fletcher 94, M. A. Piercy 93, E. L. Robinson 8.8, L. Fairclough 85 ;_total 550.</p>
        <p>_ Faretiam Company, 3rd Hants.—A. Woolridge 94, E. Edmunds 94, A. S. Gales 92, H. Tatford 92, E. J. Holdaway 90, E. May 84; total 516.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT,</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 12th November</p>
        <p>Sunday.—Mayor's Parade, Parish Church, Warrington. Fall-in Stafford-road 9.30, leave Town Hall 10 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Monday.—Company and Recruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45.</p>
        <p>Tue;day.--tecruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. Thursday.--Recruits' Drill and Signalling Class, Headquarters' 7.45. Friday.—Swedish Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. FRANK WARBURTON; Lieut., Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>Authorities at the Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (in the chair) and other magistrates. When arrested he stated that he "wouldn't have been here now, if I hadn't been out of a job," and that he "wouldn't go in the Army if he -could find work." Prisoner now stated: "I can't help being born in this. country." The Chief Constable: "That's the country's misfortune."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>DRUNK AT SEVENTEEN.</head>
        <p>DRUNK AT SEVENTEEN.</p>
        <p>A boy of seventeen was charged, at the Borough Police Court on Monday, with being drunk and disorderly in Sankey-street. The Chief Constable said the boy's father did not know he took drink, and the case was adjourned for a month, the boy to be put under the care of Mr. Laws, the Police Court Mis- sioner, and to ilign the pledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>LIGHT DANGER.</head>
        <p>THE LIGHT DANGER.</p>
        <p>The following were fined at Tuesday's Borough Police Court for failing to have lights in their premises properly shaded :—William Caldwell, 46, Wakefield-street, 205.; John Leigh, 123, Liver- pool-rd., 205.; Wm. Martin, 3, Bennett-st., 205.: Alexander Fraser, 31, Stafford-road, 205.; Richard Bennett, 20, Causeway-avenue, 10s.; Phcebe Yates, 20, Heathside, ss. A Magistrate's Criticism.</p>
        <p>Among the defendants at Thursday's Court wits Mr. William Roberts, a magistrate, who vacated the Bench to answer the summons: If was stated by the policenian who called at Mr. Roberts's boot shop, in Bridge-street, that the defendant said: "It is a bit thick for a few minutes." The officer said he would have to report the defendant, who thereupon replied: "Well, do the same all round." The defend- ant was four minutes after time.—Mr. Roberts said he was exceedingly sorry the offence had been committed, as all along he had 'given 'strict instructions to his assistants that the Lighting Order should be observed. On this occasion they were' only four minutes behind according to the Town Clock.—The Chief Con- stable.: "I hope your Worships won't accept the Town Clock as being right."—Fined 20s. Other defendants fined were :—Maria Massey', Bridge-street, 205.; Lee and Clark., Bridge- street, 205.; John Griffiths, Walton-road, Stock- ton Heath, 10s.; Alice Bate, 24, Queen-greet, 10s.; Arthur Hadcroft, 90, Hardy-street, les.; George Bayliss, 653, Knutsford-road, 10s.; Win' Rab'bitt, 7, Cobden-street, 55.; and Victor Powell, 92, Hardy-street, 56.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>ANOTHER WONDERFUL RECORD. Zam-Buk Cures Four Generations In One Family.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER WONDERFUL RECORD. Zam-Buk Cures Four Generations In One Family.</p>
        <p>Eczema, Wounds And Scalp Sores.</p>
        <p>To have fought ,in the Crimea sixty years ago, and to-day be "doing his bit,' at the age of 85, as a recruiting agent, is the proud and patriotic record of Mr. GeO. Smith, an ex-Sergeant of the Royal Marines, who resides at 36, George-road, Burlington-road, New Malden, Surrey. Another record of which the veteran is very proud is that no fewer than four'generations in his family have experienced the wonderful healing power of Zam-Buk.' Chatting to a London journalist, Mr. Smith showed how indispensable Zam-Buk is. lie said:—</p>
        <p>"I have used Zam-Buk on many occasions in recent years for cuts and sores, and have never known it to fail. Zam-Buk acts like magic, even on my old flesh." Mrs. Mitchell, the veteran's daughter, next gave her testimony to the invaluable proper- ties of Zam-Buk as a family, healer. Another occasion on which Zam-Buk revealed its won- derful healing powers was when Mr. Smith's great grandson, Leonard, a child of five years, suffered from eczema.</p>
        <p>"Little pimples broke out at the back of his neck," said the boy's mother, 'rand spread over the crown of his head. The irritation caused Leonard to scratch the itchy, white-headed pimples, which then broke into running sores, the discharge from which led to other bad places. We tried ever so many remedies from the chemist, but .might just as well have saved our money. Leonard's head was. terribly 'bad. There was quite a hole in his neck, so deeplY did the eczema eat in.</p>
        <p>"Y wish I had had the good sense to try' Zam-Buk at first, for as soon as I did so, the result was astounding. Zam-Buk dried up the discharge, soOthed the irritation, prevented the eruption spreading, and in a comparativelY short time completely' healed Leonard's dis- tressing skin trouble. We all consider it won- derful the way Zam-Buk rooted out the eczema,, and made my boy's skin beautiful and healthY• Then Mrs. Miller, Mr. Smiths second daugh- ter said :—" I have also used Zam-Buk for years. My son, Ernest, had to go into hospital with a sore head, but came out 'without being cured: However, we turned to Zam-Buk and that completely clued Mil, as it had done Leonard." Why Mary Did It.</p>
        <p>Mistress: "Mary, how is it I see you walking' out with a soldier when you've got a young In" in the trenches?" Mary: "Well, Mum, I only does it to take Off the bare look."--" Punch."</p>
      </div>
