      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY LIitOCARDELL ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL'FLYING A*7_ SERIAL FlLht- BY . ALE C J . BRA 1,134.</head>
        <p>A PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY LIitOCARDELL ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL'FLYING A*7_ SERIAL FlLht- BY . ALE C J . BRA 1,134.</p>
        <p>[Characters in this story axe purely imaginary, living</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXW. THE MAD MILLIONAIRE.</p>
        <p>Excitement ran high as the bids mounted for the great diamond. "Are you all through, gentle- men?" asked the auctioneer. "I am offered one hundred and seventy thousand dollars for this mag- nificent diamond. Its previous history is unknown, but local experts have pronounced it to be worth ten times the sum you offer. Where did it come from? That I cannot tell you, but by the decree of the court it is adjudged an asset of the bankrupt Santley circus. As .such, the purchaser's title will be valid. Am I offered any more? One hundred and seventy thousand . . ." Homer Graydon, wealthy spendthrift as he was, grew ashen faced. One hundred and seventy thousand dollars would cramp him to raise, but Vivian Marston gave him an alluring smile, and, as his pride was also at stake, he made an effort to smile back at Vivian and nodded to John Powell as if to say, "Go higher, if you: can." The rival bidder laughed recklessly. Once again he felt all the daring carelessness of .his wild youth as Arthur Stanleigh, of Stanleigh Hall. "One hundred and eighty thousand!" he cried. Vivian was in a flutter of unholy delight to realise she was the inspiration of the money-mad rivalry between John Powell and Homer Graydon as they eagerly bid for the bauble that had been a talisman of death. _ _ _</p>
        <p>" One hundred and.eighty thousand I am bid for by Mr. Powell. Come, come, Mr. Graydon, do not let superstition stop you. The lion would have killed the lady, and the jealous husband would have killed Sam Santley, even had this diamond not figured in the case. All great diamonds have their dramatic history. Remember the Koh-i-noor and Orioff diamond. This diamond may be a lucky stone, for all we know, despite shat has hap- pened." Arthur knew better. Blair knew better, and so did Vivian and the English lawyer. Smythe. So did Durand, the international diamond thief and swindler. And so did his accomplice and jackal, the dapper Count de Vaux. But Homer Graydon only knew that Vivian Marston's favour depended on the diamond. _ _</p>
        <p>"One hundred and eighty-five thousand dollars!", he cried; then he gulped and grew .ashen pale again. To raise that :amount of cash to pay for this bauble to give a woman would be his ruin. "One hundred and ninety thousand," cried John Powell.</p>
        <p>Homer Graydon gave the auctioneer a negative jerk in replying to his inquiring gaze. "One hundred and ninety thousand! Going, going, gone! Sold to Mr. John Powell for one hun- dred and ninety thousand dollars!" Arthur scribbled a cheque as the crowd buzzed and swayed around him. Vivian gave a hysterical laugh, and clutched Arthur by the arm as the auctioneer handed him The Diamond from the Sky. Then, as the 'purchaser and his little' coterie of friends passed throUgh the dispersing crowd. Vivian looked Romer Graydon deliberately in the face and turned her back upon him.</p>
        <p>In her ioom at her modest hotel Esther debated nervously with herself. The rank injustibe done Luke who had been sent to prison by the influence of Blair Stanleigh and Frank Durand, while Arthur had never interfered, made her more bitter than she had been at the strange attitude Arthur had adopted towards her since his recovery from his injuries. Rumours that his mind was affected as the result of his hurts were rife in Los Angeles, where those who had formerly called him "the Golden Man" now designated Arthur as "John Powell. the mad millionaire." For herself she would never again intrude upon Arthur, surrounded as he was by syncophants and parasites, .who worked him evil. But for Luke Lovell, done a great injustice, she would make one last appeal before she returned to Richmond and Hagar. The remorse and contrition of Luke that he had ever been disloyal to her and -Hagar she felt was genuine. She determined to take the English lawyer,, Smythe. into her. .confidence, and found occasion to send Quabba to Smythe With a message. Lawyer Smythe was also preparing to leave Los Angeles. He had shipped his precious deer head by express with much misgiving and foreboding of harm falling it ere it reached his dearly-beloved England, where he was shortly to follow. Like Esther. the eccentric but loyal English lawyer was becoming discouraged and dismayed at the puzzling conduct of the American heir to the Stanleigh Earl- dom. Here was the heir, a fugitive from justice in Virginia, flourishing under the name of "John Powell," a thousand lia,gues from Stanleigh Hall, Virginia. "I confess it is an enigma to me," he explained to Esther. "Of course, Blair Stanleigh, the next in succession, cannot become the Earl of Stanleigh so long as Arthur Stanleigh lives. That Arthur Stanleigh lives under another name only adds to the confusion of the whole thing." Then Esther told him about Luke, and because he had a kindly heart in his queer body the Eng- lish lawyer accompanied Esther to the prison on a visiting day. They found Luke contrite and tender both towards Esther and his gipsy sovereign, Hagar. He assured the girl that once he was again out of prison and had his revenge he would return to the tribe and serve Hagar faithfully as head nian again. But he was obdurate in his sullen determination to have his revenge in his own way. "Go back to Virginia, Esther," he said, "and leave the matter of my enemies to me. They have so much influence a poor gipsy could get no jus- tice against them in the courts. If I told my story ,it wouldn't be listened to. But I can't be held here for ever. and •I will' have revenge in a better way."</p>
        <p>In this -trame of mind, mingled with his tender attitude towards her. Esther and the English lawver left him. Luke looked strange and grim with his cropped head and shaven face in his prison garb. He seemed another being, a graven-faced, im- placable personification of a vengeance that could wait to strike. .</p>
        <p>Hardly had the newspaper sensation of John</p>
        <p>&gt;rer,t,efneeet;'ecetee,</p>
        <p>and .have no connection with or relation to any erson.l</p>
        <p>Powell's purchase of the diamond died than "the mad millionaire" broke into print again. Now the newspapers announced that to celebrate re- covery from his recent injuries, Mr. John Powell would give a grand fete, the first formal hoUse- warming of his grand new mansion. At this fete, the papers stated, the great diamond that had caused the tragedy and, bankruptcy of the Santley circus would figure in a sensational surprise. It was Vivian who had suggested the fete, a cos- tume affair. Arthur, tormented as he was with the devilish suggestion of insane delusions, and addicted to evil drugs, welcomed any excitement,' any dissi- pation, to make him forget the attacks of remorse that ever and anon obsessed him. Like many others in the same situation. Arthur believed his evil companions were his good friends and true, for they flattered his vanities and fawned upon him. Vivian lived in an elysium of reckless and costly vanity. There were high revels nightly by the mad millionaire and his friends in the gay resorts of Los Angeles, and now there was to come the crown- ing effort in wasteful and luxurious prodigality— the Powell costume ball. Arthur abandoned all ,business affairs tu -Blair. Now that he had The Diamond from the Sky, Vivian was exerting every *He to obtain it, and Arthur promised he would present it to her fittingly at the fete.</p>
        <p>But there was no honour among thieves. Already Durand was planning to cheat Blair, -after Arthur was ruined. Durand broached this to Vivian, who played her ,cards -tactfplly, and seemingly encour- aged him in his treachery to Blair. Her one desire was the diamond, and that was to be hers the night of the fete. She could hardly wait for it. Esther had decided the beat way to intercede for Luke would be to intrude upon Arthur at the fete, and make him promise to secure Luke's release from prison.</p>
        <p>Smythe accompanied Esther in a taxi-cab to the fete. He was attired as a knight in armour, but persisted in carrying his great English umbrella. He explained he did not wish to get wet and become rusted in his armour in case it rained. When informed that the rainy season in this section of the country was yet some months off, Smythe had re- plied.jhat rain knew no seasons. He had seen it rain na London when even the " Times " had pre- dicted fair weather. So he would run no risk and would take his umbrella. At the Powell mansion, entering with the crush of dancers, Esther and Smythe found the butler arrayed as a gorgeous major domo receiving the guests, as, it was whispered, the host was to make his entrance in some novel and surprising manner. In fact, the Costume fete was a lavish Bohemian revel. Wine flowed, punch was served on every side, and 'the guests made themselves at home and joined in the dances and revels. A band played in the grand ballroom, and a string orchestra in the Conservatory. Attentive servants were at every hand giving care to the guests. Smythe, a humoresque figure with his belmet and armour and his incongruous crook handle umbrella. led,Esther to theone quiet spot in all the bedlam of romp and revelry. This was a small upstairs re- ception or tea room, plainly but richly furnished,' and with a large window that overlooked the lawns and flower garden. A wide-branched palm in a handsome jardiniere screened a small divan or settee near the centre of the room. Beside it was a small ornate table. A great door led to the billiard- room, and through the billiard-room gave ingress and egress to the quiet spot. A few couples strayed out and in; but for the most part Esther was alone in this small tearoom after Smythe left her to apprise the master of the house that a lady wished to see him there.</p>
        <p>In the great ballroom the gay set of Los, Angeles were hilarious amidst the music. Blair, a hand- some and striking figure in the full uniform of a Confederate ofcer. forgot his schemes and plans, and enjoyed himself with all the abandon of his reckless nature. Durand, arrayed in a convention- alised costume as the King of Diamonds, looked like that court card come to life.</p>
        <p>"I am running true to form," whispered he to Vivian.' ' " ' The Pack called me the King of Diamonds, and here I am. you see, costumed for the part."</p>
        <p>"You wouldn't have dared to dress in character abroad," laughed . Vivian. "The police of Europe look too constantly for the King of Diamonds." "You are right, my dear girl," replied Durand, carelessly. "But, speaking of Europe, even the carnival at Monte Carlo was no gayer scene than this. Certainly our friend, the whilom Sohn Powell, is giving a princely fete. But what is this big sur- prise he is going to spring?" "I haven't the least idea," confessed Vivian. "He is very secretive of late."</p>
        <p>Then her brow knit into a slight frown as she remembered that Arthur had promised to give her the diamond that -night; but when she had sent him a note by her maid, he had only returned her word that the diamond would be given her later in the evening. Where was he? What was his surprise? The climax of the fete was reached when John Powell rode into the ballroom on a white horse and told the company how an ancestor found The Diamond from the Sky. 'When he had finished the story a footman brought him word that a lady wished to 'speak to him in' the little reception room. He was followed by Vivian, still waiting for the surprise of the night. There he found Esther wait- ing for him. With a cry, she pushed him-back into a chair, and as Arthur held the diamond out to Esther, Vivian grasped at his sleeve, tearing the rich embroidery. Divining that she would inter- fere, Arthur pushed Vivian out of the room, lock- ing- the door upon her.</p>
        <p>Then he turned to Esther with the gem, but at that moment the torn sleeve revealed the syringe marks upon his arms, and with a cry of anguish the girl ran to the window and flung the diamond out into the garden. Blair, Durand, de Vaux, and the enraged Vivian burst into the room, the girl crying, "Where is the diamond?"</p>
        <p>"Out there," replied the bewildered Arthur, pointing to the open window. (To be continued.)</p>
