      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIELD.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>..) • .:. • 4 4t)•:••:••:••:••:•+•:••:-.:-:-:••.7.4.4-).:••:-.:••:. •:•.:..:.+.:.•:.•:-:-:•401:••:•.:-:••:••:••:••:-.).:••:•4. 4*</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>LEFEATED AT HOME.</p>
        <p>Warrington's First Lapse At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Who was the mystery man at Wilderspool on Saturday? Stan. Young! Since he joined the Army he has had his hair cut, and we could hardly recognise him.</p>
        <p>He came down from his training quarters with Ernie Brookes, who • is in the same battery.</p>
        <p>There was .a agate" of 3,ooo—the largest of the season—to watch the encounter with Salford. As an exhibition of general running ,and scrambling the match would have required some beating, and a stranger to the game—and possibly many of the wounded soldiers accom- modated on the stand were in that category— would have thought at -times that he had landed in Madrid instead of Wilderspool. For the greater part of the game play was unduly rough, with the result that the quality of the play suffered.</p>
        <p>The result of Saturday's match just about represents the difference in the teams, in so far as Salford took the opportunity which pre- sented itself ad through John, who both scored the try and converted it, won the match. Dur- ing the whole of the game there were oaly about three- good passing movements, and these were . . executed by the visitors. Perhaps the lo,kql men could *get' along— a little better if they adapted themselves more quickly than they do. For the reasons stated above good football was discounted, but really our men ought to be able to take in a situation more readily than they manifested on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Although there was a lack of cohesion and rhythm in the team, one factor which con- tributed against this was that the three-quarters did not line out properly, for instead of their oblique alignment they seemed to be dotted here, there, and everywhere, one at the front, another at the back, and never in correct position, with the result. that if a player had a ball he could never count on sure support. With this error in the taking up of positions is allied their failure to practise inside passes and licks. Once in particular, Baker, having drawn the whole opposition, - was surrounded by at leant four players, and, instead of just kicking to the centre of the field and thus giving the home forwards a chance, he clung to the ball and allowed himself to be bungled into touch., The backs were inconspicuous through- out the afternoon.</p>
        <p>Comment on iedividuals would be out of the question from a football standpoint, but if one were asked to name the outstanding man on the Warrington side on Saturday, then that man was Cunliffe, for he was head and shoulders over the others. His dribbling was a treat to witness, and if he had been, better supported, notably on one occasion., when he and Launcb were having a neck and neck race for the line with the ball loose, perhaps there would have been something numerical to record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>MATCH IN DETAIL.</head>
        <p>THE MATCH IN DETAIL.</p>
        <p>Warrington won the toss and Salford kicked off, Woods placing the ball half-way towards the War- rington line. The Salford threequarters indulged in a nice pass near the centre line, but Warring- ton tackled successfully. Potter received from a scrum and passed to Brookes; who made slight headway, but stepped into touch. The Warring- ton forwards broke away, and another scrimmage was formed almost under the Salford line. The ball was passed out .to the right, and Baker was within an ace of getting over. Clegg robbing him very neatly. Tranter broke away and bluffed the opposition. He was successfully dealt with, and Clifford getting possession with an open field before him ran half the length of the ground before a Warrington player overtook him and brought him to earth with the aid of a slip on the part of the Salford man. Brookes and Baker got the ball under the Salford goal, but Launce was equal to all de- mands. and Salford did another breakaway, which ended in nothing. Woods made a brilliant run for the visitors, which ended in a dangerous-looking scrimmage in the home "25." Salford gained ground stubbornly, and a scrum on the Warrington line was only made safe by a free kick against the visitors. The Salford men were very keen, and Warrington were having all they could do to keep them out. Play became rough in a vigorous melee on the Warrington line, and the, referee spoke to a Salford player. A minute later John forced his way over, from a crowd of players and kicked a goal. From a mark at long range Potter tried for a goal, but was short. Potter and Brookes were making tracks for Salford territory, but a forward pass ended a promising mode. Young got through several Salford men, but was unable to make fur- ther headway. "Thomas, receiving the ball after a brilliant passing move by the Salford threequarters, looked a certain scorer, but Baker got him by the ankle at the very last moment.</p>
        <p>-- Half-time: Salford, one goal one try (five points) ; Warrington, nil. • The second half opened with an effort by Dainteth to score from a penalty kick, and he missed scor- ing by a few inches. Launce mulled near the Sal- ford line, and Baker had a grand opening, but he only placed the ball in touch. 'Play got on ,the rough side again, and Salford were chiefly on •the aggressive. Ainsworth stemmed a rush by War- rington and from the ensuing scrum Salford kicked away recklessly to save a threatening situation. Warrington got the ball over the line, but Launce beat Cunliffe for final possession. Final: Salford, one goal one try (five points) ; Warrington, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
