      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WARRINGTON AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Warrington travel to St. Helens to-day to meet the Recs., and will select their team from :—Hor- rocks; Baker, Tranter, Potter, 'Brookes, Griffiths; Nicholas, Dainteth, Young. Douglas; Harmer, Green. Cunliffe. Matthews, Jones, Wall and Dutnbell.</p>
        <p>COLITI:TION FOR CHRISTMAS</p>
        <p>PUDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Cenerous Offer Of The Warrington</p>
        <p>Commi t tee.</p>
        <p>A week to-day, ,during the Warrington and Oldham match, there will be a collection on the Warrington Football Ground for the fund for supplying our soldiers on, active service in all parts of the world with a Christmas pudding on Christmas Day. Every sixpence contributed will supply one man.</p>
        <p>The delivery of plum puddings sent by indi- viduals is not guaranteed this year. This collection is made by "The Examiner" as part of the official War Office scheme, and the delivery of the puddings is guaranteed. There are many footballers doing their duty abroad. Those at home will doubtless do their utmost to supply them with this little seasonable cheer.</p>
        <p>Our thanks are due to Mr. T. P. Bradshaw and his committee for their kind offer in this matter; arid to the Chief Constable for his per- mission to allow the collection to be made.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE. Result of Match played last Saturday. Rylgnds's, 5 points; Heathside, 2 points.</p>
        <p>Match To-day. Rylands's v. gt. Thomas'. At Wilderspool; kiek, off 3 o'clock. Referee, Mr. G. Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>Results to Date.</p>
        <p>GOALS.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D.' F. A. P. Trasimere Rovers Hurst South Liverp00l Walkden Central Warrington Town Altrincham Stalybridge Celtic Eccles Borough - 8 2 3 3 16 17 7 Barrow Atherton Hyde</p>
        <p>and Sunderland player, hut finished badly. Kite had scarcely a decent shot to deal with. Their defence wets excellent, especially Johnson and Smith, the goalkeeper\ The half-backs and for- wards did not combine at all well; this is evi- dently due to the fact that they are only a new organisation. Bradshaw worked very hard, and if his play lacked finish he was the best of the trio, the wing men affording very little protec- tion for the backs, Turner being very .often .in difficulties, and could not master Banner and Woodhouse. Johnson, the left back, played a game—a good game. Of Smith's goalkeeping we cannot 'speak too highly. Although beaten five dines, he was faYfrom being to blame, as many shots he had saved were wonderful, and had he failed to save he could not have been blamed. The Town forwards were at their best, and played real football, the understanding between them and the half-backs being excellent. One, therefore, can sympathise with the home de- fence. Aspinall played well and ably led the attack. Although he failed to find the net, he made a few- good attempts and took a good part in the goals scored. He passed the ball with excellent judgment from .fight to left. He was generally in the right place and worried the opposition. Siddall, too, was very promi- nent, and succeeded in finding the net three times. He is a' splendid forward, if at times rather slow. He can shoot well, and when on ,the target invariably finds the net. Eccleston, the extreme right winger, was only moderate. Banner and Woodhouse are developing into a good pair; their combination was pretty and caused the home defence much trouble. The latter pair were ably supported by Ditchfield, and none played with such vivacity, skill and power. He held the home .team's right wing with a tight rein. Whitfield and Stephe4; the -other half-thiCks; played exceedingly,well.. Leah and Millward, the full-backs, acquitted them- selves most creditably. They are playing a great game. As already stated, Kite had hardly a shot to (teal with. In. fact, the Town will re- quire some • beating on their present form. Teams:—</p>
        <p>Lancaster: Smith; Turner, Johnson; Parkin, Bradshaw, Clancy; Wardroper, Gladstone, Stockley, Xiclfarrow and Ellis: Warrington. Town: Kite; Leah, Millward: Stephens, ,Whitfield; Ditchfield; Eccleston, Sid- dall, Aspinall,. Woodhouse and Banner. Scorers: Siddall (3), Ditchfield and Banner, for Warrington; Ellis, for Lancaster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>ASSOCIATION NOTES.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION NOTES.</p>
        <p>Thecommittee of the Town Association Foot- ball Club, having received applications from a good many local junior players desirous to have a trial in the Combination team, decided to run a reserve 'team to enable them to train and develop young players to fill any vacancy that may occur in the ranks of their first team. An application for membership to the St. Helens and District League has been successful, and the opening match was played last week on the Hood-lane ground, Earlestown West End being the visitors.</p>
        <p>The constitution of the Reserve team was not one that would inspire confidence to the players, or would it enable their committee to judge most of their players as to their abilities. If you had asked the players who turned out which position they would prefer to play in, you would most certainly have got an answer frNin them differing from the position they were placed in—centre-forward playing outside-left, centre-half playing inside right, inside-forwarOi playing full-back. The goalkeeper was prob. ably the only player who was playing in the position he usually occupies when playing for his own club. Yet in spite of all these draw- backs the team as a whole acquitted themselves fairly well. To be defeated by the odd goal under such circumstances was not surprising, if not altogether as good as we should have liked.-</p>
        <p>In the Junior League there was a surprising transformation in the display of the Highfield Juniors against the Grappenhall team on the Tannery ground. The visitors opened very strongly, and scored twice before .there was any response from the home players, and the interval score was two goals each. It was in the second half -tfiat the Juniors gave their best display of , the season. They fairly monopolised the game, the visitors beiiig com- pletely outclassed. The Juniors made their total score to eleven goals against three.</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors have succeeded in making satisfactory arrangements to play their fixtures on the King and Queen Ground, Padgate-lane, and on Saturday they had Howley United as visitors. The home team were represented by their usual eleven, but the visitors were not able to put their strongest team in the field; having, during the week, lost the services of lloarty and Hinds, two of their most capable half-backs, both having been called up for the Army. Good reserves were obtained, and an easy victory was obtained by the United by four clear goals.</p>
        <p>The keenest match of the day was played in the Park betvieen the Recs. and the Hornets. The game was strenuously contested right up to the call of time, when the verdict was in favour of the Recs. by the odd goal in five. This was a good performiance of the Recs., owing to their being short of four of their regular players, namely, Lockhart; Green, Brown and Broadhurst.</p>
        <p>Widnes St. Mary's ,entertained the Bewsey. Adult School on the County Ground, and in a game in which many easy. chances Were missed the "Saints ", were only, successful by the nearest margin--one goal to none. This was their first victory in the League, and judging from the display of the " Saints " during the game, more sting in the final efforts of their forwards will bring them further victories in the near future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>•J L.</head>
        <p>•J L.</p>
        <p>On Saturday St. Elphin's had Widnes Juniors as visitors to Steel-street ground, and the ".Saints,",encouraged by the victory of the pre- vious week, were in a scoring mood. Playing with confidence, they were returned easy winners ty five goals against one, and have now taken the lead in the League Table fOr the first time this season.</p>
        <p>Highqeld made a big advance in their goal average at the expense of Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>Widnes St. Mary's have struck a winning patch.</p>
        <p>Widnes 'Juniors are the only team without a win to their credit.</p>
        <p>There is sure to be a keen struggle to-day be- tween the Juniors on the Tanhouse-lane ground.</p>
        <p>Another keen match in the Park, Cadets v. Hornets.</p>
        <p>Hoartv and Hind of Howley, and Crannigan of the Cadets, have responded to the call and joined up.</p>
        <p>THE TOWN AT HOOD LANE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town are at home to-day to Hyde, and will field the 191 lowing team: Kite, Leah and Millward;, Whitfield, Standfast, Ditchfield; Stephens, Siddall, Aspinall, Woodhouse and Banner,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>LANCASTER FACTORY v. WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>TOWN.</p>
        <p>The. Town.*iiited- Lancastei- last Saturday to play a friendly match with the Factory team, which includes many well-known first-class players. It was a disappointment to the spec- tators when it was announced that Hodgson and Nesbit, of Burnley, could not assist them, owing to the match between those old local rivals. Burnley and Blackburn, and those players' services were required by their old club.</p>
        <p>The Town won by five goals to one, Siddall performing the "hat trick"; and had it not been for Johnson (Preston North -End) Wand Smith (Bolton Wanderers' goalkeeper); the Factory would have suffered ai much heavier defeat. The rest of the team played extremely hard, but the Town were better in every- depart- ment and gave an all round exhibition a pace, vigour and Skill 'that greatly pleased the 500 spectators.</p>
        <p>The afternoon was pleasant and ideal for a good 'fast game, The Town made Only one change from the team that did so well in de- feating Liverpool Reserves by 8-2 the previous week, Siddall taking the place of Standfast, who could not get away from his Work. , The Town started with ASpinall leading the attack, and for some time play was very even and in mid- field.. The home, team opened the score after about twenty minutes of play, through their centre-forward, Ellis, of Burnley fame. It was an exceedingly good piece of work,, though the goal ought never to have been scored, for it was the result of some bungling on the part of Mill- ward and a misunderstanding by Leah. The former 'failed to clear a centre, when Ellis dashed through, and Kite had no chance with the shot. This reverse seemed to " liven " tte Town players, and very soon did they assert their authority, A.spinall paying marked attention to his left wing, who seemed to have the opposing half-back completely "bottled." After a splen- did run of the former be passed out to the left outside, who raced down the touch and centred with a nice long "daisy-cutter?' Aspinall, at at- tention, sent the ball with a crash against the cross-bar. From the rebound it was cleared and put up the field, Whitfield retur,ning to Siddall, who made no mistake, and screwed the ball into the corner of the net to equalise matters; Lancaster had started very well, ably led bv Bradshaw, the ex-Notts Forest, Preston N.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>he monthly meeting df the above League was held last Monday night at the Peter-street Dining Rooms, Mr. R. Simpson presiding. With one ex- ception all the clubs were represented. The treasurer's report showed the finance of the League to be in every way satisfactory. A protest from ‘Penketh against the Appleton Hornets was decided in favour of the latter. A protest from Orford Juniors against the result of their match with Howley United on the ground that the latter club played two players without' haying obtained their transfers according to rule was upheld, and two points were deducted from the Howley Club and given to the Orford Club. The two,players were suspended for four weeks.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mrs. M. E. Price thanking the club for the collections, which amounted to £2 65., on behalf of her son, who is a member of the Latchford Recs. Club, and had the misfortune to have his leg broken a few weeks ago. He is get- ting on very satisfactory, 4nd hopes to be out of the Infirmary this week. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>ST. HELENS &amp; DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS &amp; DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RESERVE v. EARLESTOWN WEST END.</p>
        <p>The opening /Mitch- of the Town Reserve team in the above competition was played last week on the Hood-lane ground, when the following players lined out:—</p>
        <p>Town Reserve: G. Lockhart; Stubbs, Boyd; Davis. Midge, Pritchard; Deakin, Green, Hankin- son, Drake and Joe Lockhart.</p>
        <p>West End: Hollis; Sherlock, Riley; Hughes, Sherlock, Morse; Derricot, Crawley, Gee, Silver- wood and Anderton.</p>
        <p>Referee,. Mr. Worrall, Sutton</p>
        <p>The visitor); advanced on the home goal. Lock- hart having to kick away in the first minute. Then off-side against Hankinson sent the ,home forwards back. End to end play ..followed for a while, and good play by J. Lockhart resulted in an attack on the visitors' goal. Deakin returning the ball to the goalmouth, Drake accepted the opening and scored for the home team, after ten minutes' play. F.`rom the centre kick the visitors made attempts to equal- ise. Stubbs and Boyd 'defended well until Ander- ton kicked wide from a neat pass by Gee. Several other attempts on the home citadel were eventually cleared, and at the opposite end Sherlock and Riley proved equal to the home forwards and play was again in the home half. Anderton tested Lockhart with a long shot, but in the next minute Silver- wood received from Derricot and made the scores equal. The visi•-• now showed improvement in their play, and had the best of the eachangcs, an 3 Gee, beating the backs. sent the ball past Lockh-irt and gained the lead for the visitors. A spell of rven play followed, after which the visitors again toc•I; up the offensive, and Anderton scored the visitors' third goal. Immediately after this the same player centred finely, Lockhart saving in neat styles Play was in the visitors' half when the in-</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere In boxes, labelled Is-3d and 3s-Od.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>. ._ – ... _ -,- • ~.( , : . . 1,-. .• :7.7, ...11110507, 4 Iffi if ' ~r: ,16. \- .' it! ~ 1 ~. , ?,. i:,7• • – • +•• Niki....,-; i.----'1•:•."1. ‘, s . 1 -.." ~ • :dili, '.. ' `'."4lL, – -%. ---,' \ ' '"•• .0" • • 7t..., .• " , ', . . • . . . • • .... ‘...--.........,4</head>
        <p>. ._ - ... _ -,- • ~.( , : . . 1,-. .• :7.7, ...11110507, 4 Iffi if ' ~r: ,16. \- .' it! ~ 1 ~. , ?,. i:,7• • - • +•• Niki....,-; i.----'1•:•."1. ‘, s . 1 -.." ~ • :dili, '.. ' `'."4lL, - -%. ---,' \ ' '"•• .0" • • 7t..., .• " , ', . . • . . . • • .... ‘...--.........,4</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL v. SANKEY ATHLETIC.</p>
        <p>In the Victoria Park. The Sankey forwards put up a strong attack on the home team's goal, but H. Wright cleared well. The home team's forwards could do nothing but hit the crossbar and uprights. The Sankey backs put their forwards in possession, and they scored after ten minutes. Later they scored their second goal. Half-time: Sankey, 2 goals; Emmanuel, nil. In the second half Sankey were awarded a free kick, from which they scored. Emmanuel tried hard to score, but failed. Sankey forwards made a strong attack and scored. Five minutes off time S. Hughes gave Eaton the ball, and he scored. Two minutes later Eaton scored a splendid goal, which left Gleave helpless. Final: Sankey, 4 goals; Emmanuel, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>GOALS. P. W. L. D. F. A. P. St. Elphin'a *Orford Juniors *Howley United ... 9 •7 1 1 28 7 13 Latchford Recs. Penketh Juniors Bewsey A.S Highfield Juniors Appleton Hornets Widnes St. Mary's Latchford Cadets Grappenhall Widnes Juniors</p>
        <p>*Howley United have had two points deducted for pliying ineligible players. and two points have been added to the *Orford Juniors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>ORFORD JUNIORS v. HOWLEY UNITED. On the King and Queen ground, before a fair crowd.</head>
        <p>ORFORD JUNIORS v. HOWLEY UNITED. On the King and Queen ground, before a fair crowd.</p>
        <p>Orford, Juniors: Conley; Gibson, Griffiths; Aus- tin, Witton, Bothwell; Chisholm, Buckley, Hurst, Clare, ancl- Belshaw,</p>
        <p>Howley United: Winstanley; Gough, Forster; Davies, Miller, Rowlinson; Broadhurst, Fitzgerald, Brown, Hassall and Humphries. Referee, Mr. J. Wright, An attack was made at once on the home goal, Conley clearing smartly from Hassall. A visit was paid to the Howley end, but Hurst lost the ball when favourably. placed.. The backs cleared and Humphries raced away on the left, teat all oppo- sition, and opened the score for the visitors with a good shot. The visitors kept up the attack and the home defence being hard pressed, Griffiths kicked through his own goal. The home team in- creased their efforts and made a raid on the visit- ors' goal. Chisholm shooting over the bar from a good position. Nearing the interval the visitors again attacked the home goal and Hassall scored a fine goal, and at half-time the score was: Howley United, 3 goals; Orford, nil. The second portion opened in favour of the United, but Gibson defended stubboinly and re- pulsed the efforts of the visitors. The Juniors were not playing up to their usual standard and did not cause much trouble to the visitors' defenders. The play was chiefly in the home half, and after a spell of uninteresting play Chisholm and Buckley broke away on the right. Winstanley, leaving his goal. 'just succeeded in clearing. Humphries scored and even play followed till the final whistle blew. Howley United, 4 goals; Orford Juniors, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>HIGHFIELD v. GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>HIGHFIELD v. GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>On the Highfield ground. H ighfield: Johnson; Hughes, Bell; Wishlade, Hankey, Jepson; Moulsdale, Pritchard, Horton, Ford and Tomlinson.</p>
        <p>rappenha II : Marshall; Aikenhead, Rigby; Rut- ter, Jones, Mason; Willoughby, Smith, Knight, Watson and Kenwright. Referee, Mr. J. Smith, jun. The visitors ,set a fast pace and quickly put the home lot on the defensive, Willoughby opening the score for them after a few minutes' play. ,The home team tetaliated strongly, and Moulsdale was a little too high, and Ford missed from a penalty kick. During another attack on the home goal Johnson gave a fine display between the uprights, and Bell finally cleared. Then the visitors were awarded a penalty kick, from which Knight did the needful. After these reverses Tomlinson showed fine form for the home team and scored their first goal. The home team continued to have the best of the play. Jepson's final shot was too high, and from a corner kick Marshall cleated well with a crdwd of players round him. Poor play in the visitors' defensive lines enabled lioulsdale to score the equalising goal. Interval: Two goals each. The home team gave a very , much improved dis- play in the second half, and quickly assailed. the visitors' goal. Tomlinson scoring after three min- utes' play. From a corner kick, well placed by Tomlinson, Mar.shall misjudged the ball and it glided into the net. The visitors could not make. headway against the home defenders, and Ford and Horton added goals. The visitors made a rush to the home end and gained a corner kick, and the ball was well placed in front of goal and put through during a scrimmage. Hughes scored from a penalty kick for the home team, who continued to have all the best of the game, and further goals were added by Tomlinson, Pritchard and Moulsdale, the final score being: Highfield, 11 goals; Grappen- hall, 3 goals. -</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD RECS. V. APPLETON HORNETS.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park. before a good crowd of specta- tors.</p>
        <p>Recs.: Kirkham; Brandwood, Wilcox; Trainor, Cooper, Bancroft; Heesom, Dutton, Warburton, Fai,rbrother and James.</p>
        <p>Hornets: Millar; Harrison, Fozard; Thornton, Howard, Chidlow; Smith, Tegg, Brown, Green and Woolley.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. A very fast pace was set and the home team had to defend, a free kick giving them relief. After a, visit to the opposite end the visitors, by smart com- bination, returned to the attack, and Green scored their first goal. Each end was quickly visited, but the defenders cleared well; then the home team made a strong rally, and Dutton made the scores equal.. Both teams became excited in their play, and tho game was not very interesting. Interval: Recs., 1 goal; Appleton', 1 goal. On resuming, the game was contested very keenly and the Hornets made. desperate attempts to gain the lead, and for a while the home defence pre- vailed, but finally had to succumb to a fine shot by Woolley. The home forwards took up the attack, and after several efforts had been defeated, Dutton suceeded in making the scores equal. Each team gained corner kicks and free kicks without avail. Following midfield exchanges, the Recs. took un the attack, and Warburton gained the lead for the Recs. Result: Latchford Recs., 3 goals; Appleton Hornets, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>WIDNES ST. MARY'S v. BEWSEY A.S.</head>
        <p>WIDNES ST. MARY'S v. BEWSEY A.S.</p>
        <p>On the County ground</p>
        <p>The opening stages were in favour of the visitors, after which the " Saints " took up the aggressive, and Holloway and his backs were hard pressed, but eventually jcleared. Later, after Holloway had cleared, Cooper returned with a long shot and scored for the home team. The "Saints" con- tinued to have the best of the game, but failed to accept their chances. Half-time: St. Mary's, 1 goal; tewsey A.S., nil.</p>
        <p>_ Resuming, the "Saints" had much their own way in midfield, but' when near goal their fine efforts</p>
        <p>were weak. Many were wide of the goal. McAllis- ter missed an open goal and Gregory shot wide from a penalty kick. The visitors were well represented by Holloway in goal and his backs; the rest of their team were dilly in moderate form. Final score: St. Mary's. 1 goal; Bewsey A.S., nil.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S V. WIDNES JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>On the. Steel-street ground</p>
        <p>St. Elphin's: Povey; Tunstall, Furness; Cooper, Miller, Gleave; Watson, Shelmerdine, Lee, Jones and Holden.</p>
        <p>Widnes Juniors: Boyle; Fern, 14arah; Mooney, COIN Price; Baldwin, Gilhooley, Magee, lfurt`y and Hodkisson.</p>
        <p>The Juniors opened with a strong attack, but found the "Saints'" defence to be sound, and Jones, gaining possession,-transferred to the visitors' end. Shelmerdine dribbled finely and beat Boyle with a low shot after five minutes'.play. Magee and Bald- win made good efforts to equalise, but found Furness in good form and' they had to retreat,. Watson' shooting well from the touch line, Boyle 'had diffi- culty in saving. Baldwin was next prominent for the visitors, and gave Povey a warm shot to clear. Jones, securing just inside his own half, made a good individual effort, and sent in a grand shot, which ROy.le was unable to negotiate. Half-time: St. Elphin's, 2 goals; Widnes Juniors, nil. On resuming, the Juniors forced the pace and gained three corner kicks in quick succession, but they failed to improve on them. After even play, the visitors returned to the attack, and Povey was penalised for carrying. The shot was wide, but owing to an infringement had to be taken again, and Mooney ,scored at the second attempt. The " Saints" now made tracks for the visitors' , goal, and a neat pass by Holden was nicely converted by Shelmerdine. After this the " Saints " had the best of the exchanges. Miller was playing a great game at centre-half. Holden scored the fourth goal from a free kick. Jones was prominent with fine drib- bling. but did not shoot often enough. Lee added the fifth goal a few minutes before the call of time. For the " Saints," Povey continues to keep a good goal, and Furness was the best of the backs. The half-backs all played well, but the forwards did not play up to their reputation, Jones standing out. well above his colleagues. For the visitors, Boyle showed weakness in goal. The. backs and half- backs played' fairly good. and Magee. and Baldwin were the best of the forward line. s</p>
        <p>_ Result:. St. Elphin's. 5 goale; Widnes Juniors, 1 goa4.</p>
      </div>
