      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1916.</p>
        <p>Shops, Businesses, Sze.</p>
        <p>with me yesterday, and thanked me fot, the good work I have done out hem, and my officer has recommended me for the D.C.M. I expect to be home on furlough very shortly now, and I expect to get another stripe in A day or two." Corporal Jones, who is thirty-six years of age, and a native of isialpas, Cheshire, enlisted just two years ago, and has .been at the Front about nineteen months. For ten or eleven years he was employed on a gas furnace at the works of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd. His brother, Ernest Oswald Jones, is serving at Salonica.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>SECOND HONEYMOON.</head>
        <p>A SECOND HONEYMOON.</p>
        <p>A Visit To Soidier-Husband In</p>
        <p>Switzerland.</p>
        <p>Separated since the very-beginning of the war —two years and three months—Mrs. Matthews, at present residing with her sister, Mrs. Tatlock, at 15, HaMelds-road, Orford, has just returned home from a visit to her husband, .I'rivate J. T. Matthews, South Lancashire Regiment, who is interned as prisoner of war in Switzerland. This interesting trip was, it is hardly necessary to say, thoroughly enjoyed by Mrs. Matthews, and the unexpected arrival —because it turned out somewhat of a surprise —gave their joyful meeting in Leysin some- what of a second honeymoon character. First of all, Mrs. Matthews' desire must be observed to convey publicly her thanks to Mr. T. H. Spinks, who so ably manages the concerns of the South Lancashire Prisoners of War Fund in the Parr Hall, and who kindly arranged for her journey. She has the distinction of being the first soldier's wife from Warrington to be facilitated in seeing her husband so far from home—a five days' journey. Before detailing her experiences, it should be stated that before the war Private Matthews had spent seven years in the Army—he is 33 years of age—and left in the reserve in 1913. Living. •at Winifred-street, Warrington, he worked for some time in Messrs. Crosfield's, subsequently going down to Gravesend, where he was employed in W. T: Henley's Telegraph Works Co., Ltd. On the order for mobilisation, 2nd August, 1914, he came up to .the depot in Warrington to join his regiment, and on the memorable Friday night,. when the South Lancashire Regiment left amidst such enthusi- astic scenes—he was in the number who went to France. • How many of these brave fellows never returned? Private Matthews was through the first awful shock of arms, and it</p>
        <p>PRIVATE AID MRS. MATTHEWS. Photographed in Switzerland.</p>
        <p>was on or about the 23rd October, 1914, when he was wounded—and lay on the field not -for ' hours, but days. His wounds were severe—a ' bullet in the left arm and another wound in the right leg—which another bullet completely pierced. He was taken to a Belgian hospital at Namur, and subsequently remained in German hands. Private Matthews did not tell his wife very much about his experience in the German camp, but what he did say was quite enough. He was always very ill, as, in addition to his wounds, he suffered from chest trouble, and when men were unable to work the Germans made them dig holes and place pieces of wood in the form of crosses into them. To these they were tied for long periods of time and left practically without food. Nevertheless, -the prisoners continued cheerful and helped each other all they could. Broken in health and apparently, with ninny others, only a burden to ,the Germans, Private Matthews, to his great delight, was sent to • Leysin, in Switzerland—a beautiful health resort, 4,500 feet above sea level. There he is stopping in a hotel and having as his room mate Private Campbell, of the South Lanes., who resided in Liverpool. IN THE SERGEANTS' MESS.</p>
        <p>Turning to Mrs. Matthews' journey to her prisoner 'of•war, she says that she had a four days' wait in London, and then sailed via Southampton to Havre. Then to Paris, where they rested a few hpurs, and were well cared for. They had another long rest on the way to the frontier at Montreaux, where some Eng- lish ladies were most attentive and asked the women all about their husbands, showing the greatest consideration. . Although Private Matthews knew that his wife was coming to see him, the exact time of her arrival was not stated. When, accordingly, Mrs. Matthews and Mrs. Ames got to the Hotel Beau-Site, where the men are billeted, both husbands had gone out for a few hours' stroll to enjoy the "rest " cure. So they were entertained, to tea in the Sergeants' Mess by 'Sergeant Watson and Mrs. Watson. And in- this delectable situation did the truant husbands find their better halves- on their return. Oh, what a surprises and what a meeting! How many sad-hearted women wOuld not give half a lifetime for such an experience? The next fourteen days passed over too swiftly. The couples were accommodated— in a clia!et some distance • from the hotel; and the happy time was spent in mountaineering and enjoying' the marvellous air and scenery of Switzerland. Mrs. Matthews left Leysin on. the 2nd Novem- ber, and on the return journey a stop was made in Paris to se the sights. She brought back some flower from Switzerland, a rose, ivy, edelweiss and a trefoil, which Private Matthews christened a Swiss shamrock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>CKURCH AND NONCONFORMISTS.</head>
        <p>CKURCH AND NONCONFORMISTS.</p>
        <p>Rev. T. T. James' Reply To The</p>
        <p>Rector's Claims.</p>
        <p>UNREAL "RE-UNION" TALK</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln a speech reported in your last issue the ,Rector of Warrington claims that the Church of England is "the one and only true branch of Christ's Holy Catholic Church in this land." If I do not deal with any arguments On which one may conceive this assumption to be based, it is not because the Nonconformist posi- tion does not rest upon spiritual conviction ,and ordered thinking, but because this seems the least fitting of all times for religious controversy. Yet it does appear to me that the occasion should not pass without the reminder that no true Nonconformist is ever likely to abate his belief that the Church to which he belongs is a true branch of the Church of Jesus Christ, or to surrender his assurance as to the validity of its ministry. Two comments you will allow me to make on the words reported. First: It may well be an aspiration in many minds that these days of crisis shall bring all branches of the Church of Christ closer together, but such a claim as this, made on behalf of the Anglican Church, only tends to show how unreal a thing much of our talk about the re-union is, and how truly diffi- cult it is likely to be to get any steps taken in that direction.</p>
        <p>Second: The assumption itself- may, seem harmless enough as a theory, deeply as one may disagree with it. The actual results' which spring from it, however, are sometimes of an unfortunate character. Convinced Free CAurch- m4n possess a passionate belief that the Church they love is verily to be described as "the body of Christ," and if the Church of England could realise how keenly they resent the persistent entry of young curates intck their homes on an errand of proselytising, it would show some ability to understand Nonconformity and the spiritual conviction for which it stands. I write with no narrow prejudice against the Anglican Church, but with a sincere apprecia- tion- of its work and the service of its priest- hood. lam glad to count myself amongst those li-ho regard the Rector of Warrington with feel- ings of honour and respect, but those feelings are sometimes best maintained when men speak clearly what is in their minds.—Yours. T. T. JAMES. Wycliffe Congregational Church, November 9th, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>Mantles and Furs.</head>
        <p>Mantles and Furs.</p>
        <p>With winter at our doors and even one foot across the threshold, it would he wise for ladies to note that Messrs. Lee and Clarke's fine showrooms at 4 and 6, Bridge-street, are now open. This latest extension of promises accommodates a display of mantles and furs and a vast variety of rain-coats, winter cloth- ing, flannel and linen goods. The hosiery department is also fully stocked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>MILITARY MEDAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY MEDAL.</p>
        <p>on Of Ex-Policeman Decorated By</p>
        <p>General.</p>
        <p>A son of that popular ex-policeman, Mr. William Clarke, of 15, Enville-street, Latch- ford, was decorated with the Military Medal by a General, on the battlefield last Sunday. He gained it on the Bth August, when digging a trench under the supervision of Lieutenant Coury, who gained his V.C. at the time. When the General had pinned the ribbon on his breast he shook hands with Private Clarke, and remarked that it was a well and hard-earned medal. The recipient replied, "I only did my duty."</p>
        <p>The new Military Medalist is 26 years of age. It is said he was the first to re-engage in the South Lancashire Regiment when mobilisation was ordered. He has spent twenty-two months at the Front, during most of which time he acted as a 'bomber, but latterly has been engaged as a stretcher-bearer. He went through several important engagements. Private John Clarke was first employed at</p>
        <p>R.ylands Bras., Ltd., afterwards going to the engineering department at the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. He was educated at St. :limes' School, and was a meinbee of Kent-street Mission. With his uncle, Mr. .1:- B. Starkey, he ,helped to start the Latchford Cadet Corps, and was a member of. the Order of Druids.</p>
