      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>s'IGNALLER T. ELLISON.</head>
        <p>s'IGNALLER T. ELLISON.</p>
        <p>‘‘'rites To Say He Has Won The Military Medal.</p>
        <p>Another Warrington man 3warded the Military Medal is Signaller Thomas Ellison, .R.F.A., son of Mt and Mrs. I% Ellison, 3, Priestley-street. Writing home, he says be has been awarded the honour and been conv.ratu- lated by his officer. hut does not give ang details as to how he won it. Signaller Ellison, who enlisted in March of last year, and went to the Front eleven months ago. has five cousins with the Australian Contingent. Hp was forinerly .employed by the Whitecross Co.. Ltd., .and is an "old boy." of Arpley-street S‘hool. He also -attended St. Luke's Chu-A:II, Liverpool-road, and is 23 years of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>011</head>
        <p>011</p>
        <p>RENT REDUCED.</p>
        <p>By Messrs. Greenall, 'Whitley And Company.</p>
        <p>A generous act by the landlords to a butcher who has gone into the Army gave rise to some interesting points at a meeting of the Assess- ment Committee at the Workhouse; yesterday. Mr. 11. B. Houghton appeared and asked the committee to reduce the assessment on his client's butcher's shop in the. Market, as he had enlisted. When he went in August the landlords, Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., lowered the rent from £4O to £2O per annum until his return, and as the assessment still stood where it did-240 gross and £34 rateable— this was considered to be to high. The Chairman (Mr. W. Furness) asked hoiv the shop would let if it became vacant? Mr. W. H. WOodcoek (assistant overseer) re- marked that, judging by the way shops were letting now, the owners would not get £4O a year for it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said it was all right for a man to have generous landlords, but, from their Standpoint; they wanted to know how much the property would let at. Mr. Houghton replied that he had grave doubts whether it would let at all at the pre- sent time if it became vacant. Considering the patriotic motives of the landlords, he thought the Assessment Committee might see their way tp reduce the assessment for the time being.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Mather: But we are hot dealing with our own money,- but with ratepayers' money.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We are not here just as patYiots. We must consider things on their merits.</p>
        <p>The committee, by the casting vote V the Chairman, decided to reduce the asessment to vn gross.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>GRAND OPERA.</head>
        <p>GRAND OPERA.</p>
        <p>Amidst the dull round of life—its trials and and anxieties—Warrington has been favoured with a week of harmonies. This happy inter- lude has been occasioned by a return visit of the llarrison-Frewin Opera s..lompany, and the Royal Court Theatre has overflowed at each performance ,with entranced ' and delighted audiences. It is a rare pleasure to have an opportunity of hearing such a striking com- bination of musical talent, and Warrington amply showed its appreciation in exceptional numbers and unwonted enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>The week's programme represented a gener- ously selected bill of fare--there was variety without satiety—.the viands were dainty and full flavoured, and .the wines of excellent bouquet. No course was repeated. Every section of music-lovers was catered for. Verdi, Bizet, Gounod and Mascagni for the gourmets of intricate 'composition; Wallace, Balfe and Leoneavallo for what may be described with- out offence as the more popular taste. This does not sugest that the more impressive art of "Ii Trovatore " • and " Faust " passed un- heeded by ,thle multitude. The house was packed on each occasion and applause was un- stinted; while the delightful skill and com- pleteness of Mascagni's prize work was received with as much satisfaction as if each hearer was intimately versed in all the mysteries of musical structure.</p>
        <p>Of the leading members of the company, much indeed might be written, and it would be a special pleasure to dilate upon their many excellencies, but "art is long and time is fleeting," and the stage is too small to hold all the floral tributes. Versatility in opera is a gift from the gods, and Miss Raymond° Amy is specially favoured. How wide apart are Maritana and Leonora, and yet how successful she proved, and not only here but as Santuzza in " Cavalleria," and Nedda in " Pagliacci." But, undoubtedly, her highest excellence was</p>
        <p>shown in the most exacting music of "II Trovatore." Her purity and range were ex- quisite--her poignancy of expression brought a pain into the heart. This great opera—trivial perhaps in some small choruses and somewhat tiresome in iteration in the, same—still 'remains- one of the greatest tests of vocal endurance, and the manner of its production on Thursday night marks at once the striking talent which Mr. Harrison-Frewin commands. With Mr Furness . Williams as Manrico, Mr. Lewys James as Count de Luna, and Miss Christine Oliver, the gipsy mother, this was indeed a notable performance. Mr. Furness Williams was, shall we say, immense; and with the ex- ception of a tiny failure on one exacting note --due: probably to smoking in the Circle—he bore the strain right through with astonishing ease and completeness. Further, he is an accomplished actor, and these fine qualities were also displayed as The Duke in." Rigoletto." Miss Christine Oliver, of whom more anon, was a tragic Azucena, and her voice and acting superb. Seldom have we heard that tear- compelling duet, • " Home to our mountains," more appealingly rendered. This splendid cast was completed by Mr. Alex Jones (Fer 4 rando), Miss Marie Lewis (Inez), and Mr. Harry Lawn (Ruiz). Mr. Lewys James, so dramatic and holding in the character of the Count de Luna, achieved yet a greater success aa Rigoletto in the opera of that mine, while his Tonio in " Pagliacci " was an equally finiFhed production. Equal revelations of talent were forthcoming in the production of "Faust "—anoth?.... aly of stars. with Miss Nora D'..4.rgel as the par- ticular orb—a richly sweet and difightful Marguerite, whose pure soprano shoite with ex- ceptional brilliance. With such a Faust as Mr. John Harrison, she could not be otherwise than supremely happy. This magnificent tenor was so rich in the tenderest pasasges; so easily strong in the emphasised phrases that the whole beauty of his score was equtilly dis- played. One. of the finest appearances on the operatic stage in the part of Mephistopheles was that of Mr. Robert Parker, whose declama- tory powers and vigour of expression single him out for special reference in connection with this production. His saturnine humour, in the garden -scene with Martha (Miss Marjorie Laurence), was characteristic. As Escamillo in "Carmen," his rendering of the famous Toreador song won unstinted -applause. When the curtain rose on Carmen we scarcely ex- pected to hear and see such a magnetic prima donna as 'Miss Christine Oliver. A• more fault- less presentation of the gay, .alluring, un- scrupulous cigarette-maker could not be desired. Her mezzo voice is abundant in expression and her dramatic qualities reach a very high level. Mr. John Clarke, who had been such a dhing Don Ctesar in " Maritana," was a marked suc- cess as Don Jose in Bizet's master work. A bright and pleasing contrast is the singing of Miss Nora D'Argel, whose numbers in the part of Michaela were rendered with great charm and sweetness.</p>
        <p>Of the rest, whom shall we set down? Miss Esther Yunson, such a delightful Lazarillo in " Maritana "; Mr. Tom Griffiths, Don Jose in the same pleasing opera; Miss Ella Rees, the Siebel of "Faust "; or Mr. Harry Reynolds, the Valentine, *whose ability and training were equally conspicuous in other works. In a word, the company is having a triumphant week, and well deserving it. The choruses are most creditable and, if the male side lack robustness and commanding vigour on occasion, the reason is not far to seek. - For the band there is nothing but praise. Of fairly large proportions, the balance of tone has been skil- fully arranged and maintained. In descriptive passages and, perhaps more particula'rly, the horrific movements in portions of II Trovatore, the mind was irresistibly carried with the flood of emotion. Mr. Harrison-Frewin is a great leader—his orchestra was swayed by the most delicate movement of the 'baton.</p>
        <p>To-night Balk's evergreen onera, "The Bohemian Girl," will be produced, with Miss Ella Rees as Arline; Mr. Furness Williams, Thaddeus; Mr. Tom Griffiths, The Count; and Mr. Harry Reynolds ag Devilshoof. There is sure to be a "bumper house. Meanwhile we thank the Harrison-Trewin Opera Company for their visit, and heartily wish them " Au revoir."</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre. Those striking pen and ink sketches which have attracted the patrons of the Royal Court Theatre as they moved about the corridors and up and down the stairs this week have been excellent advertisements of the " Tiger'S• Cub," which is being presented next week. It is a, play with an exceedingly good reptitation, hav- ing been produced at the Garrick and Queen's Theatres, London, where it rim for , over 200 nights—a great testimony to its power and popu- larity. " Tiger's Cub" has been delighting Manchester this week, and is a worthyrisuccessor to the fine productions by the Harrison-Trewin Opera Company now in possession at the Royal court. A romance of Alaska, it has novelty in its favour. It is full of vitality—a strong play in the exact sense of the phrase. On Saturday afternoon next there will be a matinee in aid of" Our Day." That success will attend it is assured, for the programme is mag-, netic. Mr. Harold Raynor, Miss Ennis Lawson and Mr. John Blake are to appear in "Love v. Law," a comedy which has . never been pre- sented before to a Warrington audience. Mem- bers of the Harrison-Trewin Opera Company are also coming from St. Helens. The Warrington, Drainatic Society are coming out of their war- time retirement to produce one of Barrie's charming "half-hours "—" The .Twelve Pound Look." - Palacq and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>It is by no means .an easy matter to come across a true comedy sketch, and most produc- tions, which are sometimes' so described, are overdone in the direction of farce. This (1111710 t be said with regard to , the charming little episode staged by Shirley and Ransome, entitled "The Separation Order." The plot, although exceedingly lender, gives much opportunity for effective work, and the players who take the parts of a Cockney coster and his ,wife, who become reconciled, do not miss a point. • The Courtiers, in an original musical comedy scene, introduce a number of " catchy " songs, which are very successful, especially a number named "Sympathy." which 'is exceptionally well received.. Their humorous sketch, dealing with the difficulties of a stage manager at "rehearsal," introduces a great amount of fun. For a really pretty entertainment commend me to that given by the Twelve Rainbows, a galaxy of talented girls, whose songs and dances are .enlivening and .graceful. They make a very dainty and attractive troupe. Dancing may have a very unusual aspect, and this was amply proved by the performance of Lean Lynn, Who</p>
        <p>N md"</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
