        <p>LANCE-CORPL. J. CARTY. "Cannot Realise He Has Been Killed," Says Comrade. Mrs. Carty, 96, Sharpe-street, has been in-</p>
        <p>i..)rmed that her son, Lance-Corporal Jame:: :arty, Rifle Brigade, has been killed. A cora- ade, Rifleman W. Davies, has written: " I feel the going of Jim as badly as anybody, as,he and were. always together. I cannot realise that 'le has been killed. He was by my side when it happened. The bullet went into his heart and he died instantly." Lance-Corporal Carty, who enlisted in Sep- tember, 1914, and went to France in January of last year, formerly worked on the oil plant at Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd.</p>
        <p>Private 'Thomas Fisher, whose. widowed mother resides at 10, bolman's-lane, off Bridge- street, has had a.remarkable escape from death at the Front. He has forwarded to his home three photographs, which, • along with a prayer-book, were pierced by a shrapnel bullet. lie writes to his mother as follows:— "I am glad to say I went all through the S mime battle with just a scratch. I am send- ing you a few photos, which are souvenirs. I was struck by a small piece-of shrapnel on the left pocket of my tunic, and, -lucky enough, I always carry my prayer-book in that pocket. It pierced almost right through the photo- graphs, and, therefore, the book saved my life. It would have hit me in a dangerous place." . Private Fisher, who celebrates his twenty- first birthday on Tuesday, and is an only son, has been wounded three times. He enlisted in August, 1914, and has been :at ,the Front eighteen months.</p>
        <p>MISSING. PRIVATE T. JOHNSON. Eight Weeks At The Front And Now Missing. Private Thomas Johnson, King's Own Royal Lancasters, is officially reported missing, ,and</p>
