        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS JOHNSON. Missing.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED.' • SERGEANT WILLIAM KIMBLIN. Bewsey Forgeman Who Fought At Spion Kop. Sergeant William Kimblin, of the South Lancashire Regiment, whose wife and one child reside at 8, Refuge-square, Lilford-street, Bew- sey, has been twice wounded in France within the space of a few weeks. He was: wounded on</p>
        <p>September 3rd, but the injuries appear to have been slight, for he returned to duty, and by October 23rd he had been admitted to• the Canadian General Hospital suffering from gun- shot wounds in the hand and left foot. Sergeant Kimblin, who is 37 years of age, and a reservist, went all through the Boer war and fought at Colenso, Spion Kop and Tieters Hill, going through all those fierce engagements with- out so much as a scratch. Similarly he sur- vived the German onslaught at Mons and the severe fighting which followed. He worked at Bewsey Forge, and joined his regiment on the olitbreak of war. CORPORAL H. 'THOMAS. Wounded A Second Time In France. Writing from the 12th General' Hospital, -Rouen, Corporal Harry Thomas, Rifle Brigade, announces the fact that Ile has been wounded ;n the back, and is doing nicely: He is now in hospital at Liverpool. This is the second time he has been wounded, having received a wound in the leg on July 33th of last .year. Promoted from the rank of Rifleman, Cor-</p>
        <p>CORPORAL H. THOMAS. Wounded.</p>
        <p>poral Thomas is a son of Mrs. Thomas. 143, Knutsford-road, and enlisted a month after the outbreak of war. He had been in France eleven months when he was wounded. For- merly a Shunter at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., he was an old scholar of St. Jame's' School, and in his 24th year. He is also a member of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds. His brother, Frederick William, is serving with the Liverpool Regiment</p>
        <p>CORPORAL C. WALKER. Hit By Sniper After Four Months On The Somme. After four months on the Somme, Corporal Charles Walker, son of Mr. and Mrs. S. T. Walker, of 36, Folly-lane, arrived at Southport Infirmary ,on Tuesday suffering from a flesh wound received on the 31st ulto. A visit by a relative confirmed the assurance of letters that the wound is not very serious. Previous to being wounded, Corporal Walker went safely through three desperate charges. At the time he got his wound he and two men were going forward from the reserve trenches to the firing line on a special mission, when a sniper caught him, and his wound had to be dressed while he stood in mud and water well over his knees.. • Corporal Walker enlisted during the first week in January, immediately his employers, the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., granted his Meuse.. It is interesting to record that as he was three weeks in training before he was presented with his outfit, he donned khaki-just twelve months to the day that his brother relinquished it, the latter</p>
