        <p>CORPORAL C. WALKER. Wounded. having been discharged after five months' service with the South Staffordshire Regiment. CORPORAL L. WARLEY. An Irishman Who Fought With His Countrymen. A stalwart member of the Dublin Fusiliers, in the person of Corporal Luke Varley, who lodged with Mr. and Mrs. Curley, 26, Folly- lane, lies in a Stockport hospital suffering from several wounds received at the Front. He sends word that he is doing nicely. The Corporal left the Pearson and Knowles Coal and iron Co., Ltd., to join the Army at Christmas, • 1914, and, being an Irishman, proud of his country and of the military achievements of his countrymen, he joined the Dublin Fusiliers. A few months' training, and he was in France. He fought in many im- portant actions. In the last one he was wounded and lay in a water-logged trench throughout a night, during which time he bled con- tinuously. Through this his condition was so critical that he was kept in hospital in</p>
        <p>PRIVATE W., HILL. Second Time To Be IsVotmdNi In A Short Period. , A comrade of Private William Hill, Highland Light Infantry, writing to Mr. Joseph Hill, 44, , ' Hopwood-street, has conveyed the news that his son has been wounded in the hand: Private Hill, who was wounded in the shoulder some time ago, enlisted in November of last year. He formerly worked for the Long- ford Wire Company, Sand was educated at St. Ann's School, and also at a Manchester school. His 'brother, Private James - Hill, was killed during the Dardanelles campaign. , PRIVATE G. FEARNLEY., Wounded In Head, But Progressing Favourabfy„ Private George Fearnley, of the South Lancashire Regiment, .son of Mr. and Mrs. J. FearnleY, 30, Watkin-street, is in the Norfolk War Hospital suffering from a wound in the head. He writes to his parents, however, to say</p>
