        <p>4T3'</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. T. HARRISON. Wounded. they found I had a fracture of the leg." Private Harrison, who is thirty years of age, is a well-known member of the Buffalo Order, in which society he held the rank of City Marshal. He was about to be made Primo in March, when he joined the Colours, and he had been at the Front about a month. He is 'a butcher by trade, but was formerly employed at Messrs. Armitage and Rigby's Cockhedge Factory.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE H. LIGHTFOOT. . otinded Soon Alter He Had Cone Over The Parapet. Going to France seven days after celebrating his seventeenth birthday, Pr..vate Harold Light- foot, son of Mrs. sLiebtfoot, 31b, street, was wounded in a charge on October 21st (a month later), soon after he had gone over the parapet. He is still in Prance, and of the seriousness of his wound, which he received in the shoulder, his parents- have not yet been fully acquainted. Private Lightfoot enlisted in the South Lancashire Regiment fourteen months ago, and was attached to the Lewis. Gun Section,. He used to work at Jackson's pawnshop, and at- tended St. Peter's Bible Class. A bzother,</p>
