        <p>PRIVATE E. I. RICKERS. Suffering from Trench Fever.</p>
        <p>was formerly employed in the office of the Pen- keth Tanning Co. An "old boy" of Arpley- street and the Grammar Schools, be enlisted about twelve months ago, and had only been at the Front about a month when he was taken ill.</p>
        <p>ON THE CONNEMARA. Local Soldier Victim Of The, Irish Sea Disaster. Mr. Elias Lomax, 9, Carlisle-street, has been officially informed that his son, Private James</p>
        <p>_ s ; . • '4'; "t.. r'zt I mil PRIVATE JAS. 1..011,AX. Drowned. Lomax, of the Royal Irish Fusiliers, was on board the London and North-Western Railway steamer Connemara when she was sunk in collision on Friday night of last week, and that his body has been recovered. Private Lomax was coming home on a short leave when the collision occurred. In his pocket a letter was found, addressed to Miss A. Lomax, Carlisle- street, and he was probably identified by this means. Private Lomax, who was twenty-four years of age, joined the Army on the 29th August, 1914, and on the 9th August, 1915, was wounded at Suvla Bay. He was an "01(1 boy" of the National School, Church-street, and was for- merly employed in the wire ropery department of Messrs. Rylands Bros.' works. He worshipped at St. George's Mission. • His body was brought to Warrington on Thursday, and the funeral will take place to-day (Saturday) with full military honours.</p>
