        <p>terval was called, the scores being: Earlestovrn West End, 3 goals; Warrington-Reserve, I...gea/. 1 :i On resuming the home team made, several-changes of the positions of . the prayers, and an improve i ment in their attackwas immediately seen. The ' r game was contested with more spirit and at a quicker pace, and each goalkeeper was called upon to clear. The home forwards now adopted long . t • passing movements., and were seen to better ad- vantage, but Riley, Sherlock, and Hollis defended their charge very stubbornly. Midge was nearly clear of the backs, but overran the ball and lost a good opening. Good• combination by the visitors transferred to the home end, and Lockhart saved ¼••,. well from Silverwood and had to concede a corner kick. This was got away and at the opposite end i ,'.:l Illidge was at fault when well ,placed. The visitors - - now became more aggressive• and Lockhart made I two good saves and Green cleared with a big kick. . Even pTay followed and each goal had narrow • escapes,but the defenders prevailed until the last few minutes of the game, when the home forwards made 7-• a final rally and Deakin scored a good goal. Time expired before the game could be resumed, the final score being :—Ear4stown West End. 3 goals; War- rington Reserve, 2 goals. TO-DAY'S MATCHES. Penketh Juniors v. Latchford Rec.; Mr. J. Gleave. Widnes Juniors v. Highfield Juniors; Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. . Latchford Cadets v. Appleton Hornets; Mr. W. Knight. Bewsey A.S. v. St. Elphin's; Mr. T. Smith. ' Grappenhall v. Orford juniors; Mr. P. Gosling. Howley United v. Widnes St. Mary's; Mr. H. Berry. ' TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. • .. LATCHFORD CADETS v. APPLETON • HORNETS. , f 4 '. At Victoria Park. Cadets: S. Miller; S. Lunt, W. Massey; F. Plumb, S. Gibson. A. Blackmore; •S. Jones, J. Clarke, W. •• - Davenport, F. Sankey and F. Leah. Reserves: J. Mayers. G. Appleton and R. Hope. ~ LATCHFORD RECS. v. PENKETH JUNIORS. Kick-off 3 p.m. Team to meat, at Rylands-street at 1.30 p.m. L